\begin{abstract}

Computing the excitation spectra of quantum many-body systems on noisy quantum devices is a promising avenue to demonstrate the practical utility of current quantum processors, especially as we move toward the ``megaquop'' regime. For this task, here we introduce a \textit{tangent-space excitation ansatz} for quantum circuits, motivated by the quasi-particle picture of many-body systems and the structural similarity between quantum circuits and classical tensor networks. Increasing the circuit depth by one layer to construct the tangent space around the variational optimum of a parametrized quantum circuit, we show that a large number of low-energy states can be accurately captured. We demonstrate this ansatz using various models in both one and two spatial dimensions, including the challenging kagome Heisenberg antiferromagnet. We further provide evidence that this approach, implementable using Hadamard test, is stable in the presence of measurement noise and scalable to large system size.

\end{abstract}

\maketitle


\textit{Introduction.--}
Understanding low-energy properties of quantum many-body systems is of paramount importance in physics, with far-reaching applications in chemistry and materials science~\cite{Altland2010,Wen2004}. The underlying concept, quasi-particles (e.g., magnons, spinons, and anyons), represent a hallmark of modern condensed matter physics. Classical approaches to this task face intrinsic challenges~\cite{Troyer2005,Orus2014}, while quantum computing~\cite{Nielsen2012}, a new computational paradigm, holds the promise of solving ground state of many-body systems without incurring the exponential wall or the sign problem~\cite{Arute2019,Kim2023,Bluvstein2024,Acharya2025,Gao2025}. Since the early days of noisy intermediate-scale quantum era~\cite{Preskill2018}, the variational quantum eigensolver (VQE)~\cite{Peruzzo2014,Wecker2015,Yuan2019,Liu2019,McArdle2020,Wiersema2020,Cerezo2021,Stanisic2022,Tilly2022,Bharti2022,Park2024,Cao2024a} has been one of the most studied quantum algorithms that offers a potential pathway to quantum speedup. This promise has also been extended to the preparation of low-energy excited states, motivating the development of various quantum algorithms~\cite{McClean2017,Higgott2019,Nakanishi2019,Jones2019,Xu2023a}. Nevertheless, locality, a key property of quantum many-body systems, has not been considered in its full strength in quantum algorithms for excited states, and the resources for excited state computation, e.g., circuit depths, do not scale in a favorable way with the number of excited states, calling for further algorithmic developments.


Classically, with intense developments over the last three decades, significant achievements have been realized in many-body physics using tensor network methods~\cite{Schollwock2011,Orus2019,Cirac2021}. Originally developed for ground states, tensor networks have also shown their power in real time evolution, finite temperature and non-equilibrium dynamics. Notably, with locality-based tangent space method, a tensor network generalization of the single mode approximation (SMA)~\cite{Girvin1986,Arovas1988}, low energy excitation spectrum can be accurately computed in both one dimension (1D)~\cite{Ostlund1995,Haegeman2012} and two dimensions (2D)~\cite{Vanderstraeten2019a,Chi2022,Ponsioen2023b,Tu2024}. Given the structural similarity between tensor networks and quantum circuits, this suggests that a new excitation ansatz based on locality of quantum many-body system may be possible for quantum computers. Here, leveraging on the tangent space in tensor networks, we propose an excitation ansatz for quantum circuits and demonstrate its low overhead and broad applicability for quantum many-body systems. 



% In this work, leveraging on the tangent space in tensor networks, we propose an excitation ansatz for quantum circuits. We show that, on top of the VQE for ground state, with minimally increasing circuit depth by one layer, excited states, including degenerate ground states, can be conveniently obtained in quantum circuits. We will benchmark our ansatz with various examples in 1D and 2D, and show that many experimentally relevant quantities (e.g. dynamical spin structure factor) will also be within reach.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% single mode approximation
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\textit{SMA and generalizations.--}
To motivate our ansatz, we start by discussing the single mode approximation method in many-body physics~\cite{Girvin1986,Arovas1988,Feynman1954}, focusing on the 1D setting for simplicity. Given a ground state $|\Psi\rangle$ of a $N$-site translation invariant system, the SMA constructs an excited state of momentum $K$ by acting with a momentum superposition of onsite operator $G$ on $|\Psi\rangle$: $|\Phi_K(G)\rangle = \sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}jK}G_j|\Psi\rangle$, which has reasonable accuracy for low-energy single particle excitations in gapped systems. Indeed, the effect of $G$ can be viewed as creating a quasi-particle on top of the many-body ground state (e.g., magnons being created by acting with spin flip operators on ground state), which can locate on arbitrary site of a system. Further enlarging the spatial support of local perturbations (denoted as $n$ hereafter), it was proved that isolated branches in many-body spectrum can be approximated exponentially well~\cite{Haegeman2013a}.


Applying the idea of SMA to tensor networks yields a specific form of excitation ansatz. Using the injectivity property of matrix product state (MPS) representation, a perturbation with consecutive multisite support on physical spins can be approximated as perturbation in a single tensor, with which, excited states can be reformulated as perturbation on both physical and virtual indices of local tensors. Since only a single or a group of local tensors is perturbed, one can view the excited state as living in the tangent space of the variational manifold. For a review, see Ref.~\cite{Vanderstraeten2019b}.


Likewise, SMA also suggests an excitation ansatz for quantum circuits. As $|\Phi_K(G)\rangle$ depends on $G$ linearly, one can take $G$ from the operator basis of single qubit $\{X, Y, Z, I\}$, where $X,Y,Z$ are Pauli matrices and $I$ is identity operator. The resulting four states can be viewed as a basis of a subspace, where the low energy states live. (For nonzero $K$, the state with $G=I$ is a null vector. For the ease of discussion, we keep this operator in the basis.) However, the generality of SMA and the range of excited states captured is typically limited, and to improve the accuracy, one replaces the local tensor in the MPS setting by a new tensor which still forms a subspace for the excitation. As entanglement degree of freedom and local tensors are native objects in tensor networks, we now propose a corresponding improvement in the quantum circuit setting.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% excitation ansatz
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\textit{Excitation ansatz for quantum circuits.--}
On quantum computers, the ground state $|\Psi\rangle$ of an $N$-qubit system with Hamiltonian $H$ is generated by a depth $D$ unitary circuit acting on an easy-to-prepare state $|\Psi_0\rangle$: $|\Psi\rangle=U_D U_{D-1}\cdots U_1 |\Psi_0\rangle$. Here, $U_l$ on the $l$-th layer is composed by a set of short-range gates acting on non-overlapping qubits. When the unitary gates are not known \textit{a priori}, various hybrid classical-quantum algorithms have been proposed to find the optimal one, including the VQE, the variational adiabatic quantum algorithm~\cite{Schiffer2022}, and the quantum imaginary time evolution~\cite{McArdle2019,Motta2020}, etc. For the ease of discussion, we further assume that the obtained ground state $|\Psi\rangle$ is translationally invariant, which can be realized when the unitary gate $U_l$ in each layer (or two consecutive layers) commutes with translation operator. For the case without explicit translation invariance, see End Matter and supplemental materials (SM)~\footnote{In the SM, we provide fermion solution and additional data for 1D TFI model, Hamiltonian Variational ansatz (HVA) and $n=1$ tangent space excitation for 2D TFI model, HVA ansatz for spin-$1/2$ kagome Heisenberg antiferromagnet, details of excitation spectrum and dynamical spin structure factor for 1D spin-$1/2$ Heisenberg chain, and a discussion of the difference with tangent space excitation ansatz in classical tensor networks and known quantum algorithms for excited states~\cite{Nielsen2012,Schiffer2025,McClean2017,Nakanishi2019,Sun2025,Chan2025}.}.


As the basis of excitation in SMA is obtained by adding a perturbation layer on the final variational ground state, to go beyond the SMA, one reasonable way is to insert the perturbation layer between arbitrary consecutive layers of the ground state circuits, see Fig.~\ref{fig:excitation_ansatz}(a) for a schematic illustration. Moreover, the single site operator $G$ in SMA can be naturally extended to a multisite gate. With this general scheme, we now describe our ansatz.


Our excitation ansatz is defined in the following subspace. 
Suppose the added gate $G$ (located between layer $l$ and $l+1$) acts non-trivially on $n$ qubits, which can be taken from the operator basis $\{X,Y,Z,I\}^{\otimes n}$. For a momentum $K$ sector, we take a superposition to form the state
\begin{equation}
    |\phi(G)\rangle= \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} e^{-\mathrm{i}j\frac{2\pi m}{N}}T^{j} U_D\cdots U_{l+1} G U_{l}\cdots U_1|\Psi_0\rangle,
\label{eq:1D_basis}
\end{equation}
which is an unnormalized eigenstate of translation operator $T$ with momentum $K=2\pi m/N$ ($m=0,1,\ldots,N-1$). (Here for notational simplicity, we introduce the state in the 1D setting.) Then we collect states with all different $G$ in different layers to construct a subspace $\mathbb{V}$ of the total Hilbert space $\mathbb{H}$. Denoting the total number of such states as $\chi$, the states $\{|\phi_j\rangle, j=1,2,\ldots,\chi\}$ form a set of overcomplete basis for this subspace, as some of the states could be linearly dependent. Note that, the original variational ground state is included in $\mathbb{V}$, and its dimension only scales linearly with the depth $D$, while being independent of the system size $N$. Note also that, when implementing this ansatz in a quantum device, one can consider each of the configurations in Eq.~\eqref{eq:1D_basis} separately, which we discuss in the End Matter.


\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.95\columnwidth]{fig1.pdf}
\caption{Schematics for the excitation ansatz. (a) Adding a new layer with a single local gate (shown in red) at arbitrary depth of the ground state circuit generates one configuration of the low-energy subspace. A basis with a given momentum can be obtained by taking a momentum superposition of the configuration.
(b) The excitation ansatz can be viewed as living on the tangent space $T_{|\Psi(U)\rangle}\mathcal{M}$ of a manifold $\mathcal{M}$ in the full Hilbert space $\mathbb{H}$. The manifold $\mathcal{M}$ is formed by classical tensor network states determined by the circuit, where each gate is taken as a local tensor constrained to be unitary.}
\label{fig:excitation_ansatz}
\end{figure}


With the basis available, the variational excited state takes a form $|\Phi\rangle=\sum_{i=1}^{\chi} c_i|\phi_i\rangle$ with variational parameters $\mathbf{v}=(c_1,c_2,\ldots,c_{\chi})^{\mathrm{T}}$. Since $|\Phi\rangle$ depends linearly on $\mathbf{v}$, using the variational principle, the optimization of $\mathbf{v}$ can be achieved via solving a generalized eigenvalue equation: $\mathbf{H}\mathbf{v}=E\mathbf{N}\mathbf{v}$. Here $\mathbf{N}$ is the norm matrix in the subspace $\mathbb{V}$ while $\mathbf{H}$ is the corresponding effective Hamiltonian: $\mathbf{N}_{ij}=\langle\phi_i|\phi_j\rangle$, $\mathbf{H}_{ij}=\langle\phi_i|H|\phi_j\rangle$. Given the possible zero modes in $\mathbf{N}$, one then takes a pseudo-inverse of $\mathbf{N}$ (denoted as $\mathbf{N}^{-1}$) and diagonalizes $\mathbf{N}^{-1}\mathbf{H}$, through which one finds the variational energy $E$ and eigenvector $\mathbf{v}$.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% tangent space
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\textit{Tangent space of quantum circuit tensor network.--}
Above forms the basics of our excitation ansatz. Before moving further, let us discuss the relation to tangent space of the quantum circuit variational manifold.


Taking the quantum circuit as a tensor network with each gate as an independent local tensor, a variational manifold $\mathcal{M}$ is formed by the resulting tensor network state. At the variational optimum, a tangent space $T_{|\Psi(U)\rangle}\mathcal{M}$ can be constructed by taking derivative with respect to each local tensor (see Fig.~\ref{fig:excitation_ansatz}(b)), with basis generated in the following way. Consider the $i$th gate in the $l$th layer of the ground state circuit $U_{l,i}$, we replace this unitary gate by a new gate $G$ from the corresponding operator basis while keeping all other gates fixed as in the ground state. One can further enumerate all the unitary gates in the ground state circuit, do the above gate replacement, and collect the resulting states together, forming a set of (overcomplete) basis for the tangent space $T_{|\Psi(U)\rangle}\mathcal{M}$ of the variational manifold $\mathcal{M}$. Now, when the gate $U_l$ in the ground state circuit is composed by local gates, using $G=G'U_{l,i}$ one can see that adding one layer with local gate is equivalent to replacing one ground state gate~\footnote{One caveat is that the support of $G$ and $G'$ could be slightly different.}. Then taking a direct sum of all momentum sectors, it is easy to see that our excitation ansatz Eq.~\eqref{eq:1D_basis} is in fact a Fourier transform in the tangent subspace. Thus we have 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{V}=T_{|\Psi(U)\rangle}\mathcal{M}\equiv\{G\partial_U |\Psi(U)\rangle\}.
\label{eq:tangent_space}
\end{equation} 
One potential benefit of adding one layer instead of doing gate replacement is that translation symmetry can be kept explicit, and the support of added gate can be taken as a hyperparameter of the ansatz.


With the mathematical structure of the ansatz identified, we now discuss the physical heuristics. In the simplest case, the subspace $\mathbb{V}$ only incorporates the basis states where the added gate is in the last layer, which is exactly the SMA approach. With perturbations in different layers, our ansatz in fact represents a hierarchy of subspaces, generalizing the SMA. Notice that, for an added gate away from the last layer, the effect of this gate can propagate through subsequent layers of the circuit, forming a light cone~\cite{vonKeyserlingk2018,Nahum2018}. To see this, suppose the gate is added between layer $l+1$ and $l$, with the state $|\phi\rangle = U_D\cdots U_{l+1}GU_{l}\cdots U_1|\Psi_0\rangle$, which can be equivalently represented as $|\phi\rangle = \tilde{G}|\Psi\rangle$ with $\tilde{G} = U_D\cdots U_{l+1} G U_{l+1}^\dagger \cdots U_D^\dagger$. This suggests that local perturbation in the deeper layer can propagate through the whole lattice.


To see the effect of this operator spreading, we take the Hamiltonian variational ansatz (HVA)~\cite{Wecker2015,Ho2019,Wiersema2020} as an example, which mimics adiabatic evolution. In this case, an excitation in a deep layer is close to the renormalization group fixed point, where a single particle excitation is indeed localized, and this excited state is adiabatically evolved by the HVA circuit, which transforms it to the excitation for the final Hamiltonian, in particular for an an isolated band of excitations~\cite{Haegeman2013a}. Even if it is not an isolated band, a reasoning along these lines is still plausible, in particular since the excitation is variationally optimized. Later we will show through numerical examples that this is indeed the case, and with all basis states included, the tangent space excitation ansatz has much better accuracy with broader energy range captured than the SMA, suggesting that it is necessary to keep all basis intact.


Although this ansatz is inspired by classical tensor networks, several differences exist, which we detail in the SM. We further compare in the SM our ansatz with known quantum algorithms for computing excitations, highlighting the structure simplicity and physical motivation behind. In the following, we present good-quantum-number labeled variational spectra for various models, and compare them with exact diagonalization (ED) results.


