\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.98\columnwidth]{fig2_1D_Ising.pdf}
\caption{Tangent space excitation for 1D TFI model. (a) HVA ansatz for ground state, with all gates in each dashed box identical. Red arrows indicate where we insert perturbation layer. (b) A comparison between lowest four energy levels of a $16$-site system obtained via the $n=1$ excitation ansatz and ED, showing a good accuracy of the ansatz. (c) Zooming in $g=1$, the $n=1$ excitation ansatz captures the $2$ fermion excitation in the even parity sector and the $1$ fermion and type I $3$ fermion excitation in the odd parity sector, while missing other many-particle excitations. The latter can be captured by increasing $n$, as shown in (d) with $n=4$. In the inset of (c), we compare the accuracy of subspace with different layers, suggesting that basis with deep layers are crucial for this ansatz.}
\label{fig:1D_Ising}
\end{figure}


\textit{Numerical results in 1D.--}
The first model we consider is the 1D transverse field Ising (TFI) model with Hamiltonian: $H_{\text{1D}} = -\sum_{i=1}^{N} Z_iZ_{i+1}+gX_i$, where $g>0$ and periodic boundary condition (PBC) is imposed. This model has a $\mathbb{Z}_2$ parity symmetry with symmetry operator $U_{\mathbb{Z}_2}=\prod_i X_i$. When $g<1$ the model is in the ferromagnetic phase with $\mathbb{Z}_2$ spontaneous symmetry breaking order, while for $g>1$ the ground state is unique.


We adopt the HVA~\cite{Ho2019,Wiersema2020} shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1D_Ising}(a) for the ground state, with $ZZ$ gate $U_{ZZ}=\mathrm{exp}(-\mathrm{i}\beta_l Z_iZ_{i+1}/2)$ for nearest neighbor qubits $i,i+1$ and $X$ gate $U_X=\mathrm{exp}(-\mathrm{i}\gamma_l X_i/2)$ for qubit $i$, which are parameterized by $\beta_l,\gamma_l$, respectively. Here $\beta_l,\gamma_l\in\mathbb{R}$, $l=1,2,\ldots,N/2$. Note that, the $ZZ$ gates in two consecutive layers are chosen to have the same parameters, so that the spatial translation symmetry is kept explicitly. With initial state $|\Psi_0\rangle = |+\rangle^{\otimes N}$ (here $X|+\rangle=|+\rangle$) and circuit depth $D=3N/2$ ($N$ even), the HVA ansatz is known to capture exact ground state of the 1D TFI model through numerical optimization~\cite{Ho2019}. Here we are not aiming for the exact ground states and will allow small numerical imperfections for the variational ground state.


A straightforward application of the tangent space excitation ansatz (Eq.~\eqref{eq:1D_basis}), with added layer located between consecutive $ZZ$ layer and $X$ layer (see Fig.~\ref{fig:1D_Ising}(a)), reveals the low-lying spectrum of this model. It turns out that, depending on the parity sector, the excitation ansatz has different variational power in capturing excited states. Using $|\Psi_0\rangle=|+\rangle^{\otimes N}$ as the initial state of the circuit, we target the true ground state of this model, belonging to the parity even sector. Using the excitation ansatz Eq.~\eqref{eq:1D_basis} with a $n=1$-site gate, excited states can be classified into parity even and odd sectors, denoted as $|\Phi_{\mathrm{EE}}\rangle$ and $|\Phi_{\mathrm{EO}}\rangle$, respectively. On a $16$-site chain, through comparison with ED, we find that the overall accuracy of $|\Phi_{\mathrm{EE}}\rangle$ is much better than that of $|\Phi_{\mathrm{EO}}\rangle$ (see SM for data). 


To improve the accuracy for odd parity sector without increasing $n$, we find that one can change the initial state into a parity odd Greenberger–Horne–Zeilinger (GHZ) type state $|\Psi'_{0}\rangle=(|0\rangle^{\otimes N} - |1\rangle^{\otimes N})/\sqrt{2}$, targeting the first excited state of this model. Through newly optimizing the same HVA ansatz and using the $n=1$ excitation ansatz, we find that the obtained excited states in the odd parity sector (denoted as $|\Phi_{\mathrm{OO}}\rangle$) has a better accuracy than those in the even parity sector, as one may expect.


Combining $|\Phi_{\mathrm{EE}}\rangle$ and $|\Phi_{\mathrm{OO}}\rangle$, we arrive at the low energy level spectroscopy of the 1D TFI model, showing a perfect agreement with ED for a wide range of Hamiltonian parameter $g$. See Fig.~\ref{fig:1D_Ising}(b) for the $N=16$ case. Zooming in the $g=1$ case (similarly for other $g$), there are fine structures in the variational excitation spectrum, which we now discuss.


As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1D_Ising}(c), certain low energy levels are not captured by the $n=1$ excitation ansatz, while levels with higher energy can be approximated to a high accuracy. To figure out the precise nature of these energy levels, we have carried out a analytical study of the 1D TFI model using the Jordan-Wigner transformation~\cite{Mbeng2024}. Then the excited states in even parity sector can be labeled by the even number of extra occupied fermionic modes on top of the ground state. Through comparing with the fermion solution, we found that the 2 fermion excitation can be captured by the $n=1$ ansatz, while $4,6$ fermion excitations are completely missed. Similarly, for the odd parity sector, only the states labeled as ``1 fermion'' states and ``3 fermion, I'' states can be captured. This behavior can be understood from the fact that the whole circuit maps to free fermions and the $n=1$ ansatz contains at most two fermionic operators. We refer to the SM for further details.


This analysis suggests that the $n=1$ ansatz describes best the one particle excitation in the spin language. To increase the variational power, one natural way is to increase $n$. Indeed, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1D_Ising}(d), with $n=4$, the missing levels for $n=1$ ansatz can be captured well. On the other hand, we have also checked that for larger system size (e.g., $N=20$), the one particle state can still be captured with a high accuracy using the $n=1$ ansatz, suggesting that our ansatz is scalable for this type of excited states (see SM for data).


An interesting feature is the effect of perturbation in deep layers, which we have argued may help in improving the accuracy of the ansatz. To quantify it, starting the perturbation in the last layer, we define a sequence of subspace by including the basis states with deeper layers, and the first subspace corresponds to the SMA. In the inset of Fig.~\ref{fig:1D_Ising}(c), we compare the accuracy of subspace with $n=1$, using the mean error of the first excitations in all momentum sectors as a figure of merit. It turns out, for the critical point $g=1$, the error of SMA is indeed large, and including deep layers can improve the accuracy. More prominently, a sudden jump in the error is observed, after which the error is essentially on the same order of variational ground state, suggesting that deep layers are crucial in the variational power. One can also see that, the error of variational ground state is also reduced with including perturbation in deeper layers, improving that of plain HVA.


The 1D results demonstrate that the excitation ansatz has significantly better accuracy than the SMA, even with the simple $n=1$ ansatz, and can be further improved by increasing $n$. Below we show that this is also the case in 2D.


