\textit{Numerical results in 2D.--}
One salient feature of quantum circuits is that it can be applied to higher dimensional systems with minimal overhead, where the performance of tensor networks (e.g., projected entangled-pair states~\cite{Verstraete2004} for 2D systems) is largely hindered by the high computational cost. Our excitation ansatz for quantum circuits can be conveniently extended to 2D and higher dimensional systems, for which we will illustrate with the translationally invariant case.


Consider a square lattice with linear size $L_x(L_y)$ and one-site translation operator $T_x(T_y)$ in the $x(y)$ direction. Assuming the unitary gates in each layer of the ground state circuit commute with both $T_x$ and $T_y$, the basis of the ansatz is constructed in the same way as Eq.~\eqref{eq:1D_basis}, taking a form $|\phi_{\bf K}(G)\rangle=\sum_{j_2=0}^{L_y-1}\sum_{j_1=0}^{L_x-1}e^{-\mathrm{i}(j_1K_x + j_2K_y)}T_y^{j_2}T_x^{j_1}\cdot U_{D}\ldots U_{l+1}G U_{l}\ldots U_1|\Psi_0\rangle$, which is an eigenstate of translation operators with eigenvalues ${\bf K}=(2\pi m_1/L_x, 2\pi m_2/L_y)^{\mathrm{T}}$, where $m_1=0,\ldots,L_x-1, m_2=0,\ldots,L_y-1$. One difference with the 1D case is that a multi-qubit gate $G$ can have orientations. E.g., a nearest-neighbor gate $G$ can act on the $x$ or $y$ bond. It is then reasonable to include both types of basis states in the subspace.


With this setup, we now show results for the 2D transverse field Ising model on the square lattice. The Hamiltonian is given by $H_{2D}=-\sum_{\langle i,j \rangle}Z_iZ_j - g\sum_{i}X_i$, where the first sum runs over all nearest-neighbor bonds and PBC is considered. In the thermodynamic limit, this model is known to have a critical point with $g_c=3.04$~\cite{Blote2002}, separating a $\mathbb{Z}_2$ symmetry breaking phase with ferromagnetic order at small $g$ value and a paramagnetic phase at large $g$. 


Following 1D, we use a similar HVA ansatz for the 2D TFI model~\cite{Cao2025Unveiling}, where unitary gates in each layer follow the geometric locality of the lattice. The system size we consider is $N=4\times 4$, and for all the parameters $g$, we use a depth $D=5N/2$ circuit (see the SM for details). Since this model is not exactly solvable, with no evidence for exact ground states on quantum circuits, here we variationally optimize the circuit parameters to reach a ground state energy error around $1\times 10^{-(3\sim 4)}$, and then use the tangent space excitation ansatz to compute the low-lying spectrum. Here we note that, unlike the 1D case, the circuit with GHZ type initial state turns out to have no superior performance than that with initial state $|+\rangle^{\otimes N}$, except for parameters close to $g=0$ where the ground state is close to a GHZ state. Thus in the following we will only consider initial state $|+\rangle^{\otimes N}$ for this model. In addition, the $n=2$ ansatz has better performance than the $n=1$ case (see SM for $n=1$ result).


In Fig.~\ref{fig:2D_Ising}(a), we compare the tangent space excitation spectrum with ED for a wide range of parameter $g$. Similar to the 1D case, on both sides of the phase transition, the low energy spectrum can be accurately captured with the excitation ansatz. Taking a closer look at the spectrum, we find that, in addition to the lowest excitation in each momentum sector, many of the low energy excited states can be approximated, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:2D_Ising}(b). Note that, in the ferromagnetic phase, the low energy spectrum contains both a one-magnon excitation and two-magnon bound state~\cite{Dusuel2010,Ponsioen2020}, which is also captured in our result, as shown by the comparison with ED. Moreover, also for 2D, the performance of the ansatz goes well beyond the SMA approach, whose data can be found in the SM.


%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% Discussion and outlook
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\textit{Discussion and outlook.--}
To further demonstrate the usefulness of our ansatz and potential quantum advantage, in the End Matter we show applications beyond TFI models in both 1D and 2D. We show that low-lying spectrum of the kagome Heisenberg antiferromagnet can be accurately obtained, which may help in analyzing the nature of kagome spin liquid~\cite{He2017}. Moreover, using excited states we obtain dynamical spin structure factor of a spin-$1/2$ Heisenberg chain, making direct comparison with experiments on quantum materials possible.


One natural concern is about realization of the ansatz on quantum devices. In the End Matter we show the Hadamard test is ideal for a hybrid implementation, using an example with 1D TFI model. There are a number of interesting further questions. For instance, a straightforward generalization is to consider multiple perturbations separated by certain distance and time, with which a linear space can still be constructed. It is not clear whether this form is more efficient for capturing multi-particle excitations than increasing $n$ for one layer.
On the other hand, one may wonder whether the barren plateau (BP) phenomena~\cite{McClean2018Barren} would hinder the application of this ansatz. While we have mostly used the HVA ansatz in this work, the scheme can be readily generalized to other circuit layouts, where BP phenomena can be mitigated~\cite{Zhang2024, Cao2024Exploiting}. It would be interesting to study how the performance of the excitation ansatz depends on the circuit structure, i.e., brickwall, sequential~\cite{Wei2022}, parallel sequential~\cite{Wei2025}, finite local depth circuit~\cite{Zhang2024}, and circuits with measurement, feedback and control~\cite{Piroli2021, Iqbal2024, Tantivasadakarn2024, Chen2025Nishimori, Cao2025Measurement, Zi2025, Yan2025}, and see how the BP phenomena affects the accuracy. We leave these questions to future study.


In this work, we have introduced a tangent space excitation ansatz for quantum circuits, with which many of the low energy excited states can be computed in a controlled manner. We have demonstrated that the ansatz can be implemented in a quantum computer. Although generic many-body spectra are QMA-hard to compute~\cite{Kempe2006}, realistic Hamiltonians usually contain exploitable structure. Our ansatz is a heuristic protocol to leverage that structure, seeking a quantum advantage on physically meaningful instances of this problem, and making it a potentially valuable application for the upcoming megaquop era~\cite{Preskill2025Beyond}.


