    In \cref{fig:squ plots}, the minimal value, the Lagrange parameter $\lambda=K(s)$, and the $\Lrm^2$-norm of the dominant logarithmic term $\log(1+v)$ are plotted.
    The latter seems to remain bounded, which indicates a uniform $\Hrm^2$-bound for $v(s)$. In the one-dimensional case, this can be proved analytically and can be used to pass to the limit along a subsequence to obtain a weak, stationary film-rupture solution to \eqref{eq:thin-film}, see \cite[Sec.~5]{bruell2024}. However, in the two-dimensional case, we cannot obtain a uniform bound on the $\Lrm^2$-norm of the logarithm analytically. Although the numerical results in \Cref{fig:squ plots} suggest that such a bound exists, we point out that this observation might not be conclusive since the logarithm only becomes singular very close to $v = -1$. For a more detailed discussion, we refer to \Cref{sec:discussion}.
