Focusing on the differential, clearly its lower (homogeneous part) $d_k$ is also a differential so that $(\Lambda V,d_k)$ is also a commutative differential graded algebra (cdga for short). 
If  $(\Lambda V,d_k)$ is elliptic,  by the convergence of \eqref{Milnor Moore spec}, so is $(\Lambda V,d)$. Moreover, in such a case, we have  \cite[Proposition 3]{L-M02}:
