\begin{abstract}
Let $E\to X$ be a holomorphic vector bundle with a singular Griffiths negative Hermitian metric
$h$ with analytic singularities. One can define,
given a smooth reference metric $h_0$, a current $s(E,h,h_0)$ that is called the associated Segre form, which
defines the expected Bott-Chern class and coincides with the usual Segre form of $h$ where it is smooth.
We prove that $s(E,h,h_0)$ is the limit of the Segre forms of a sequence of smooth metrics if the metric is smooth outside the unbounded locus, and in general as a limit of Segre forms of metrics with empty unbounded locus.
One can also define an associated Chern form $c(E,h,h_0)$ and we prove that
the Lelong numbers of $s(E,h,h_0)$ and $c(E,h,h_0)$ are integers if the singularities are integral,
and non-negative for $s(E,h,h_0)$. We also extend these results to a slightly larger
class of singular metrics with analytic singularities.
\end{abstract}








\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
Inspired by the importance during several decades of singular metrics on line bundles in
algebraic geometry, with origins in \cite{Dem1}, several efforts have been done to study
singular metrics on higher rank holomorphic vector bundles.
In particular, such metrics appeared in the work of Berndtsson-P\u aun, \cite{BePa},
in connection with pseudoeffectivity of twisted relative canonical bundles.
Contrary to the line bundle case, it is not clear what the curvature and connection matrix
for a singular metric should be, cf. \cite[Theorem~1.5]{Raufi}. This would appear to be an obstacle for defining associated Chern
forms (or more general characteristic forms like, e.g., Segre forms), since the standard definition
is as certain polynomials in the entries of the curvature matrix.
However, for appropriate metrics, it has still been possible
to introduce such associated forms, or more precisely currents of order $0$,
see, e.g., \cite{Hajli},\cite{Ina},\cite{LRRS},\cite{LRSW} and \cite{Xia}.
In this article, we will further the study of such associated forms in more general situations.

\smallskip

Assume that $E\to X$ is a holomorphic vector bundle over a complex manifold $X$
with a Griffiths negative singular metric $h$ with analytic singularities; this means that $\log|\cdot|_h$ is plurisubharmonic (psh) with analytic
singularities on $E\setminus 0_E$, see Section~\ref{qgnsec}.
%
In \cite{LRSW}
was introduced an associated Segre form\footnote{We keep the notion 'Segre form' although in the singular case
$s(E,h,h_0)$ is in general a current (of order $0$).}
 %$s(E)$ which is indeed a current,
 that represents the expected Bott-Chern class,
and coincides with the usual Segre form where the metric is non-singular. The form $s(E,h,h_0)$ slightly depends on a choice of
smooth reference metric $h_0$ on $E$.
For a singular metric $h$, we let the unbounded locus $Z=Z_h \subset X$ denote the complement of the
set of $x \in X$ where $\log |\cdot|^2_h$ is locally bounded on $E \setminus 0_E$ above a neighborhood of $x$.
Assume for the moment that in addition $h$ is smooth outside of $Z$.
Let $h_\e = h + \e h_0$, i.e.,
\begin{equation}\label{regular1}
|\cdot |^2_{h_\e}=|\cdot|^2_h+\e |\cdot |^2_{h_0}.
\end{equation}
Then $h_\e$ is a smooth metric on $E$. Thus the associated Segre forms $s(E,h_\epsilon)$ are well-defined and
our first main result states that
\begin{equation}\label{regular2}
s(E,h,h_0)=\lim_{\e\to 0} s(E,h_\e).
\end{equation}
This further suggests that the definition of $s(E,h,h_0)$ is natural. % and also turns out to be useful.

In this paper we will consider bundles with metrics such that $\psi=\log|\cdot|^2$ is quasi-plurisubharmonic (qpsh) with analytic
singularities on $E$. The latter condition means that locally
$\psi=c\log|f|^2+ b$, where $f$ is a tuple of holomorphic functions, $c>0$ and $b$ is bounded.
We say that the singularities are integral (rational/real) if $c$ is a positive integer (rational/real number).
Such a metric, as well as the associated bundle will be called \qas\ (qpsh with analytic singularities).
For such metrics, the unbounded locus will be an analytic subset of $X$.
We extend the definition of Segre form $s(E,h,h_0)$ to \qas\ bundles $E\to X$.
For simplicity, we will assume that \qas\ metrics have integral singularities,
which is practical in order to keep track on arithmetic information.
However, almost all the results extend easily to the case of rational or real singularities,
see Remark~\ref{mainQqasRcas}.


\begin{ex}\label{modelex}
Assume that $g\colon E\to F$ is a holomorphic vector bundle morphism and $F$ has a smooth metric,
or a \qas\ metric with empty unbounded locus. Then the metric $|\alpha|^2=|g(x)\alpha|^2$
on $E$ inherited from $F$ is \qas, see Corollary~\ref{sinister}.
\end{ex}

%Many important singular metrics on a line bundle are obtained in this way.
%When $E$ is a line bundle, many important metrics in algebraic geometry are obtained
%in this way.

\begin{ex}
An example of a singular metric $e^{\psi}$ on a line bundle $L$ is given by
$\psi=\log(|\sigma_1|^2+\cdots +|\sigma_m|^2)$, where $\sigma_j$ are sections of $L^{-1}$.
It is covered by Example~\ref{modelex} via the mapping $L\to X\times \oplus_1^m\C $,
defined by $(\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_m)$. %KOLLA TECKEN!
Such metrics are important in modern complex algebraic geometry.
\end{ex}

By a smooth metric $h$ on $E$, we mean (as usual) that with respect to a local trivialization,
the matrix representing $h$ has entries which are smooth functions, and $h$ is strictly
positive definite.
We begin by considering the basic case of \qas\ metrics with empty unbounded locus, i.e., which
are still strictly positive definite, bot not necessarily smooth.
Then we have the following result.

\begin{prop} \label{propEmptyUnboundedLocus}
Assume that $E\to X$ is a holomorphic vector bundle equipped with a \qas\ metric $h$,
and assume that the unbounded locus $Z$ of $h$ is empty. Then the Segre form $s(E,h,h_0)$
associated to $h$ is independent of the choice of smooth reference metric $h_0$,
and may hence be denoted $s(E,h)$. Furthermore, there exists locally a sequence of smooth
metrics $h_\e$ decreasing to $h$ such that
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{\e \to 0} s(E,h_\e) = s(E,h).
\end{equation}
\end{prop}

If $h$ is additionally Griffiths negative, then this is a special case of the results in
\cite{LRRS}.

We consider now a general \qas\ metric $h$ on $E$.
Then, the metric $h_\e$ defined by
\eqref{regular1} is a \qas\ metric with empty unbounded locus, cf. Lemma~\ref{knepig0} (iv).
Hence, $s(E,h_\e)$ is well-defined by the above proposition, independent of any choice
of smooth reference metric.

The components $s_k(E,h,h_0)$ of the Segre form are closed
currents of order $0$, but not necessarily positive unless both $h$ and $h_0$ are
Griffiths negative. There are anyway well-defined multiplicities $\mult_x s_k(E,h,h_0)$
at each point $x$, defined in the same way as Lelong numbers, see Section~\ref{qzsection}, and they turn out to be
non-negative integers.
%We get some arithmetic control of these numbers.
Here is our first main theorem:


%Another main result in this paper is that these numbers are non-negative integers if
%the singularities are integral.

%that if $E$ is \qas, then these numbers are non-negative
%and independent of the choice of reference metric.




\begin{thm}\label{main1}
Assume that $E\to X$ is a holomorphic vector bundle equipped with a \qas\ metric $h$,
and let $Z$ be the unbounded locus of $h$.
Let $h_0$ be a smooth reference metric, and let $s(E,h,h_0)$ denote the
associated Segre form.

\smallskip
\noindent
(i) If $h_\epsilon$ is the metric defined by \eqref{regular1}, then
$$
s(E,h_\e)\to s(E,h,h_0).
$$

\smallskip
\noindent
(ii) There is a current $w$ on $X$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{ddcw}
dd^c w= s(E,h,h_0)- s(E,h_0).
\end{equation}


\smallskip
\noindent
(iii)
We have the decomposition
\begin{equation}\label{uppesittare}
s(E,h,h_0)=s'(E,h) + M^{E,h,h_0},
\end{equation}
where $M^{E,h,h_0}$ has support on $Z$ and $s'(E,h)=\1_{X\setminus Z} s(E,h,h_0)$
is independent of $h_0$ and if $h$ is smooth on $X\setminus Z$,
$s'(E,h)$ coincides with the usual Segre form of $h$ there.




\smallskip
\noindent
(iv) If $\pi\colon X'\to X$ is a modification,
then $\pi^*h$ is a \qas\ metric on $\pi^*E$, and
%$$
%\pi^*s'(E)=s'(\pi^* E),
%$$
\begin{equation}\label{sformel}
\pi_* s(\pi^*E,\pi^*h,\pi^* h_0)=s(E,h,h_0).
\end{equation}





\smallskip
\noindent (v)
If $E'\to X$ is another holomorphic vector bundle, equipped with a smooth metric $h'$,
then
\begin{equation}\label{main31}
s(E'\oplus E,h'\oplus h,h'\oplus h_0)= s(E',h')\w s(E,h,h_0).
\end{equation}






\smallskip
\noindent
(vi)
For each integer $k\ge 0 $, there is a decomposition
\begin{equation}\label{siu0}
M_k^{E,h,h_0}=S^{E,h}_k+ N^{E,h,h_0}_k,
\end{equation}
where $S_k^{E,h}$ is the Lelong current of an analytic cycle of codimension $k$, and the non-vanishing multiplicities of $s_k'$ and $N_k$ are contained
in an analytic set with codimension $\ge k+1$. The multiplicities of $s_k'$, $S_k$ and $N_k$ are non-negative integers, independent of the choice of $h_0$.




\smallskip
\noindent
(vii)
If $h'$ is a metric on $E \to X$ comparable to $h$, and $h_0'$ is a smooth metric on $E$, then $\mult_x M_k^{E,h,h_0}=\mult_x M_k^{E,h',h_0'}$ for all $x\in X$ and $k\ge 0$, and similarly if $M_k$ is replaced by $s_k'$.
In particular $S_k^{E,h}=S_k^{E,h'}$, $k=0,1,2,\ldots$.
%
\end{thm}






\begin{remark}%[Some comments]
\noindent (i) Equation \eqref{ddcw} means that $s(E,h,h_0)$ represents the Bott-Chern class of $E$.

\smallskip
\noindent (ii)
The properties (ii) to (iv) are (at least implicitly) already proved in \cite{LRSW}.

\smallskip
\noindent(iii)
Equality \eqref{main31} implies that
\begin{align*}
s'(E'\oplus E,h'\oplus h,h'\oplus h_0)&=s(E',h')\w s'(E,h,h_0) \text{ and } \\
M^{E'\oplus E,h'\oplus h,h'\oplus h_0}&= s(E',h')\w M^{E,h,h_0}.
\end{align*}

\smallskip
\noindent (iv) Notice that if $M_k^{E,h,h_0}$ is a positive current, \eqref{siu0} is precisely the Siu decomposition.
\end{remark}

\smallskip

In \cite{BePa}, there is a general definition of singular metrics of vector bundles,
and a definition of Griffiths negative metrics as mentioned above, and a definition of
Griffiths positive metrics, which by definition are dual metrics of Griffiths negative metrics
(which coincides with the standard definition when the metric is smooth).
In e.g. the references mentioned above concerning definitions of Chern and Segre forms of
singular metrics, the results have mainly been formulated for Griffiths positive metrics,
which is natural as in the smooth case, Griffiths positive metrics seem to be more frequently
of interest than Griffiths negative metrics. In this article, we consider metrics which are
generalizations of Griffiths negative metrics instead of Griffiths positive metrics.
However, by considering duals of such metrics, one obtains corresponding statements concerning
Griffiths positive metrics, cf. Remark~\ref{remLRSWcomparison}. We opted here for the formulation
in the negatively curved case, as it leads to simpler formulations, in particular with less signs
and dual metrics.

\smallskip

Recall that in algebraic geometry, characteristic classes of vector bundles such as the Chern class,
are defined as endomorphisms on Chow groups, i.e., the $\Zk$-module
of analytic cycles $\Zc(X)$ modulo rational equivalence. In some cases, as for instance
in case of a Hermitian holomorphic vector bundle, there is a canonical representative of its Segre and Chern classes.

Inspired by \cite{AESWY} we introduce a $\Zk$-module $\QZ(X)$ of currents of order $0$
that contains the module $\Zc(X)$ of analytic cycles as a submodule, if
cycles are identified with their associated Lelong currents. In particular the $(0,0)$-current $1$ is an element in $\QZ(X)$.
Any element $\QZ(X)$ of a fixed dimension has a well-defined multiplicity (Lelong number) at each point.
We also introduce certain quotient classes $\QB(X)$ that, contrary to the Chow classes,
keep local information such as multiplicities; this fact is crucial in this paper.

%These numbers that only depends on its class in $\QB(X)$. This is in contrast to Chow classes that


A second main result is that the Segre form $s(E,h,h_0)$ extends to an endomorphism
$\s(E,h,h_0)$ on $\QZ(X)$, such that $\s_0(E,h,h_0)$ is the identity and $\s(E,h,h_0) 1=s(E,h,h_0)$.
An important fact is that $\s(E,h,h_0)$ defines a well-defined endomorphism
$\s(E,h)$ of $\QB(X)$,
independent of $h_0$.
There is an analogue of Theorem~\ref{main1} for $\s(E,h,h_0)$, see Theorem~\ref{main11}.



Since $\s(E,h,h_0)-I$ has positive degree, % therefore
\begin{equation}\label{tyska}
\c(E,h,h_0)=\sum_{\ell=0}^\infty (-1)^\ell (\s(E,h,h_0)-\I)^\ell
\end{equation}
is a well-defined endomorphism, such that
\begin{equation}\label{stork}
\c(E,h,h_0) \w \s(E,h,h_0)=\s(E,h,h_0) \w \c(E,h,h_0)=\I.
\end{equation}
It is natural to say that $c(E,h,h_0):=\c(E,h,h_0)1$ is the Chern form associated with $(E,h,h_0)$.
The same definition of $c(E,h,h_0)$ appeared in \cite{LRSW} but formulated somewhat differently.

\begin{thm}\label{main2}
Let $(E,h,h_0)$ be as in Theorem~\ref{main1}.
%
Properties (ii) to (vii) of Theorem~\ref{main1} hold if $s(E,h,h_0)$ is replaced by $c(E,h,h_0)$,
except for that the multiplicities are not necessarily non-negative.
\end{thm}



As already mentioned, the starting point for this paper is \cite{LRSW} and the ideas there.
%
The regularization
\eqref{regular1} relies on results in \cite{ABW, Bl}.
%
The arithmetic analysis of the Lelong numbers
relies on ideas in \cite{Asommar}, and the modules $\QZ(X)$ and $\QB(X)$ that
are modelled on the somewhat smaller module $\GZ(X)$ and $\B(X)$ in \cite{AESWY}.



