\section{I.~Introduction}
The chemical battery was invented more than $200$ years ago, and during this time, researchers have continued to improve it, and it has now been applied to all aspects of human's lives. With the development of science and technology and the continuous growth of people's living needs, the miniaturisation of instruments and equipment has become one of important development trends, and the battery is no exception. When the size of the devices is as small as microns or even nanometers, the quantum effect dominates its performance. The concept of the quantum battery has been born during the process of the miniaturisation. Nowadays, more and more people are trying to find ways to apply quantum theories and technologies to battery research in order to sustainingly improve battery performance in all aspects. Although the chemical battery is based on much quantum knowledge, the way the quantum battery works is entirely different~\cite{F.Campaioli2024}.


The fundamental theory of the quantum batteries is first and systematically investigated by R. Alicki and M. Fannes~\cite{R.Alicli2013} in $2013$, which is based on the work given in Ref.~\cite{Allahverdyan2004}. The upper limit of the battery's ergotropy is estimated by the properties of the free energy and the Gibbs function. It is pointed out that the quantum entanglement in the collective charging of quantum batteries is helpful to obtain more ergotropy. Based on this, the research for the quantum battery has made important progress in more than a decade. Progress consists of two main aspects: one is to provide proposals for new schemes and quantum systems that can be used to create quantum batteries; the other is to study the impact of quantum resources on the performance of quantum batteries, focusing on extractable work and charging power. The new proposals included, but were not limited to, nonreciprocal~\cite{BAhmadi2024} and topological~\cite{XYLu2025} quantum batteries. The systems for supporting the quantum batteries have also been extended to ultracold atomic gases~\cite{TKKonar2022}, miniature microwave RFs~\cite{Shaghaghi2022,Rodriguez2023}, and nuclear spins~\cite{J.Joshi2022}. The effects of the relevant parameters of these systems on the performance of the batteries are investigated correspondingly. As a unique resource for quantum systems, the entanglement has become the focus of research. A subset of researchers support the positive impact of the entanglement on quantum batteries~\cite{K.V.Hovhannisyan2013, F.C.Binder2015, D.Ferraro2018, D.Rosa2020}. However, other researches show that the entanglement can also have negative effects~\cite{FLiu2025, J.X.Liu2021, JWen2025}. The entanglement of the quantum battery based on the Dicke model was revealed to benefit the coherent work but suppress the incoherent work, and it is pointed out that either the entanglement between the battery and the charger or the quantum coherence inside the battery is important for generating ergotropy~\cite{H.L.Shi2022}. Actually, whether the entanglement is beneficial or detrimental to quantum batteries depends on the specifics~\cite{JYGyhm2024, SYLiu2023}. Meanwhile, the positive role of quantum coherence as another quantum resource for a battery has been confirmed~\cite{STirone2025, FHKamin2020}. Some research even considers the environment interacting with the system as a resource for charging the quantum battery. In $2021$, the spin-chain quantum battery in an optical cavity coupled to a Markovian thermal reservoir was investigated and it was shown that the ergotropy can be extracted during the thermal charging~\cite{F.Zhao2021}. Recently, the charging system with two qubits, one of which is a quantum battery and the other is a charger, was studied so that a long-distance wireless charging could be achieved by coupling a shared non-Markovian environment~\cite{Song2024}. Using the parameters given in that paper, it can be estimated that the ergotropy of the quantum battery tends to zero when the distance between the two qubits is greater than 1.5$\mu m$. The above studies are about the qubit batteries and provide valuable data for realistic applications of quantum batteries.


In recent years, more and more attention has been paid to continuous variable quantum batteries. In $2023$, a method was proposed for improving the intrinsic charging performance and energy storage of quantum batteries~\cite{Roddriguez2023}. The method involves incorporating a catalyst system (which can be a bit or a resonator) between the battery and the charger. Numerical simulation showed that the catalyst system consumes essentially no energy. In the same year, a continuous variable quantum battery by preparing the initial state of the charger as a Gaussian state was investigated under the conditions of considering the weak coupling between the battery and the charger and exposing the charger to a Markovian environment. The relationship between the ergotropy of the battery with the dissipation coefficient and coupling constant was analyzed, and the effect of the dissipation coefficient and the coupling constant on the phase transition of the system was investigated~\cite{Downing2023}. In $2024$, a driving term $\Omega \delta (t)(\hat{a}^{\dagger}\hat{a}^{\dagger}-\hat{a}\hat{a})/2$ was used to investigate the performance of a continuous variable quantum battery~\cite{Downing2024}. It was found that this driving term not only hyperbolically increases the energy stored in the battery, but also suppresses the dissipation effect. In the same year, an approach was proposed to achieve a unidirectional flow of energy from the charger to the quantum battery~\cite{BAhmadi2024}. In the case of an open system, it achieves the goal of converging the ergotropy in the quantum battery to a nonzero constant after a sufficiently long period of time. The approach also increases the battery's energy storage capacity, eliminating the need to disconnect the coupling between the battery and the charger at a precise point in time to maximise battery energy. Clearly, the continuous variable quantum batteries have huge potential for extracting useful work.


It is well known that the mutual induction between electromagnetic fields has been widely used in many fields. In quantum optics, AC electromagnetic fields or LC circuits can be quantized as resonators~\cite{Scully2012, A.Blasi2021, Q.Xu2022}, and the wireless and remote charging can be achieved by coupling the capacitance and inductance of two LC circuits. Inspired by these theories, we have some questions: What will be the role of quantum resources such as entanglement and coherence for the continuous variable quantum batteries? In the case of wireless charging, can the distance between the quantum battery and the charger be extended even further? It has been established that the thermal charging processes of single-mode quantum battery does not allow for the attainment of nonzero extractable work~\cite{D.Farina2019}. Therefore, it is pertinent to inquire how the thermal charging of a single-mode continuous variable quantum battery would differ. In this paper, we theoretically propose the implementation of a charging scheme for the continuous variable quantum batteries. By adjusting the relative position and distance of capacitors and inductors, we can achieve the goal of wireless and remote charging with considering only the rotating or counter-rotating wave coupling, and study the role of the frequency detuning between the charger and the battery, explore the effects of the (counter-)rotating wave coupling in the system's Hamiltonian on the battery's charging to make full use of the thermal energy which is easily accessible and inexpensive to be prepared.

\section{II.~Model and solving method }
\begin{figure}[tbph]
\centering\includegraphics[width=7.5cm]{Fig1_2nd.eps}
\caption{(Color online) Scheme of a continuous variable quantum battery with wireless and remote charging based on two coupled LC circuits. $L_{1}$ $(C_{1})$ and $L_{2}$ $(C_{2})$ denote the inductance (capacitance) of the first circuit and the second one, respectively. The $L_{m}$ $(C_{m})$ is the coupling inductance (capacitance) between the two circuits. The coupling strength is determined by the relative positions of the inductors and capacitors. }
\label{figure1}
\end{figure}
The scheme of a continuous variable quantum battery with wireless and remote charging based on two coupled LC circuits is shown in Fig.~\ref{figure1}. The coupling between the two LC circuits includes two parts: one is between the inductors and the other is between the capacitors. The coupling energy between the two inductors is
\begin{equation}
H_{L}=\frac{L_{1}}{2}I_{1}\Phi _{1}+\frac{L_{2}}{2}I_{2}\Phi _{2},  \label{equation-1}
\end{equation}%
where $L_{j}$, $I_{j}$, and $\Phi _{j}$ are the self-inductance, the current, and the magnetic flux of the $j$th ($j=1, 2$) coil, respectively. Although it appears that this formula only considers the energy of each inductor, it actually includes the energy of the interaction. This is because the magnetic flux in each coil consists of two parts: one part of the flux is excited by the coil's own current and the other part is generated by the current in the other coil. That is
\begin{equation}
\Phi _{1}=L_{1}I_{1}+L_{m}I_{2},~~~\Phi _{2}=L_{2}I_{2}+L_{m}I_{1}, \label{equation-2}
\end{equation}%
where $L_{m}$ is the mutual inductance, $k_{L}=L_{m}/\sqrt{L_{1}L_{2}}$ is
the magnetic coupling coefficient. The respective currents of the two coils can be
obtained from Eqs.~(\ref{equation-2}).  After substituting them into Eq.~(\ref{equation-1}), we have
\begin{equation}
H_{L}=\frac{L_{1}\Phi _{2}^{2}}{2(L_{1}L_{2}-L_{m}^{2})}+\frac{L_{2}\Phi
_{1}^{2}}{2(L_{1}L_{2}-L_{m}^{2})}-\frac{L_{m}\Phi _{1}\Phi _{2}}{%
L_{1}L_{2}-L_{m}^{2}}.  \label{equation-3}
\end{equation}%
Similar with the derivation process of the total magnetic energy, the coupling energy between the two capacitors is
\begin{equation}
H_{C}=\frac{C_{1}Q_{2}^{2}}{2(C_{1}C_{2}-C_{m}^{2})}+\frac{C_{2}Q_{1}^{2}}{%
2(C_{1}C_{2}-C_{m}^{2})}-\frac{C_{m}Q_{1}Q_{2}}{C_{1}C_{2}-C_{m}^{2}},
\label{equation-4}
\end{equation}%
where $C_{j}$, $Q_{j}$ and $C_{m}$ denote the capacitance, the charge, and the coupling capacitance between the capacitors, and the electric coupling coefficient is $k_{C}=C_{m}/\sqrt{C_{1}C_{2}}$~\cite{L.Huang2015}. As outlined in Refs.~\cite{Scully2012, A.Blasi2021, Q.Xu2022}, the total energy of the system is $H=H_{C}+H_{L}$.


The classical electromagnetic energy can be transformed into the quantized Hamiltonian:
\begin{eqnarray}
\widehat{H} &=&\widehat{H}_{0}+\widehat{H}_{i},~~~~ \label{equation-5}   \\
\widehat{H}_{0} &=&\hbar \omega _{1}\hat{a}_{1}^{\dagger }\hat{a}_{1}+\hbar \omega _{2}%
\hat{a}_{2}^{\dagger }\hat{a}_{2},~~~~  \notag \\
\widehat{H}_{i} &=&g\hbar (\hat{a}_{1}^{\dagger }+\hat{a}_{1})(\hat{a}_{2}^{\dagger }+%
\hat{a}_{2})+G\hbar (\hat{a}_{1}^{\dagger }-\hat{a}_{1})(\hat{a}%
_{2}^{\dagger }-\hat{a}_{2}),~~~~   \notag
\end{eqnarray}
where $\omega _{j}=[ L_{j}C_{j}(1-k_{C}^{2})(1-k_{L}^{2})]^{-\frac{1}{2}}$ represents the frequency of the $j$th LC circuit. $\widehat{H}_{0}$ denotes the sum of the energies for the charger and the battery, $\widehat{H}_{i}$ corresponds to the interaction energy between the charger and the battery. The coupling coefficients in the interacting Hamiltonian are $g=-k_{L}\sqrt{\omega _{1}\omega_{2}}/2$ and $G=k_{C}\sqrt{\omega _{1}\omega _{2}}/2$. According to the classical electromagnetic theory, the values of $k_{C}$ and $k_{L}$ belongs to the range of $(-1, 1)$, and the specific values is determined by the relative position of the inductor and capacitor as well as the distance between them. The system only includes the anti-rotating coupling if $G=g$ and the rotating coupling if $G=-g$. The relative sizes of the parameters $\omega _{1}$, $\omega_{2}$, $G$ and $g$ determine the system's charging dynamics. In the subsequent discussion, the first (second) circuit is taken as the charger (battery), with the corresponding ascending and descending operators denoted by $\hat{a}_{1}^{\dagger}$ ($\hat{a}_{2}^{\dagger}$) and $\hat{a}_{1}$ ($\hat{a}_{2}$), as shown in Fig.~\ref{figure1}.


We introduce the physical quantities for characterizing the charging dynamics of our battery system as follows: $E_{1}(t)$, $E_{2}(t)$, $E_{e}(t)$ and $E_{i}(t)$ denote the energy of the charger, the energy stored in the battery, the ergotropy (i.e., extractable work), and the interaction energy which is given by the expectation value of $\widehat{H}_{i}$; $R(t)$, $S(t)$ and $C(t)$ denote the ratio $E_{e}(t)/E_{2}(t)$, the Von Neumann entropy and the quantum coherence of the battery, respectively. $R(t)$ gives the proportion of the ergotropy in the battery's energy. Using these quantities, not only the performance of the battery can be revealed, but also the effect of the quantum entanglement and coherence on the battery can be discussed. The algorithms for the corresponding energies are defined as: $E_{1}(t)=\hbar\omega _{1}\mathrm{Tr}[\hat{\varrho} _{1}(t)\hat{a}_{1}^{\dagger}\hat{a}_{1}] $, $E_{2}(t)=\hbar\omega _{2}\mathrm{Tr}[\hat{\varrho} _{2}(t)\hat{a}_{2}^{\dagger}\hat{a}_{2}]$ and $E_{e}(t)=E_{2}(t)-\sum\nolimits_{k}r_{k}e_{k}$, where $\hat{\varrho}_{j}$ denotes the reduced density matrix of the $j$th subsystem, $r_{k}$ is the $k$th eigenvalue of the battery's Hamiltonian $\hbar\omega _{2}\hat{a}_{2}^{\dagger }\hat{a}_{2}$ in ascending order, and $e_{k}$ is the $k$th eigenvalue of $\varrho_{2}(t)$ in descending order~\cite{Allahverdyan2004}. For the Gaussian state, the calculation method of the quantum coherence $C(t)$ was given in Ref.~\cite{J.W.Xu2016}. The Gaussian state can be diagonalised analytically, so $\sum\nolimits_{k}r_{k}e_{k}=\hbar\omega _{2}(\sqrt{D}-1)/2$, where $D$ is the determinant of the covariance matrix of the Gaussian state~\cite{Weedbrook2012}. The Gaussian state can be described by its covariance matrix and $\langle\hat{a}_{2}^{\dagger}\pm \hat{a}_{2}\rangle$. If the solutions of the ordinary differential equations for the closed and open systems are obtained, all of the information about the quantum battery system will be determined [See the equations and solving methods in the Appendix].


The dynamics of the Gaussian states can be obtained by $\frac{d\langle\widehat{O}\rangle}{dt}=\mathrm{Tr}\left[\widehat{O}\frac{d\hat{\varrho} (t)}{dt}\right]$~\cite{Carmichael1989}. $\widehat{O}$ is an arbitrary time-independent operator in the Schr$\ddot{o}$dinger's picture and $\langle \widehat{O}\rangle$ denotes its expectation value. The derivative of the density matrix $\hat{\varrho}(t)$ with respect to time is determined by the master equation of the system. The equation is written as follows~\cite{Scully2012}:
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{d\hat{\varrho}(t)}{dt}&=&\frac{i}{\hbar}[\hat{\varrho} (t),\widehat{H}]  \notag \\
&& +\frac{\gamma n_{th}}{2}[2\hat{a}_{1}^{\dag}\hat{\varrho}(t)\hat{a}_{1}-\hat{a}_{1}\hat{a}_{1}^{\dag}\hat{\varrho}
(t)-\hat{\varrho}(t)\hat{a}_{1}\hat{a}_{1}^{\dag}]  \notag \\
&& +\frac{\gamma (n_{th}+1)}{2}[2\hat{a}_{1}\hat{\varrho}
(t)\hat{a}_{1}^{\dag}-\hat{a}_{1}^{\dag}\hat{a}_{1}\hat{\varrho}(t)-\hat{\varrho}(t)\hat{a}_{1}^{\dag}\hat{a}_{1}],  \label{equation-6} \notag \\
\end{eqnarray}
where $\gamma$ denotes the decay induced by the environment (i.e., the thermal reservoir). For the closed systems, the decay coefficient $\gamma$ need to be set to zero $(\gamma=0)$. For the Markovian open systems, the coefficient is a nonzero constant $(\gamma\neq0)$. $n_{th}$ is the mean photon number for the mode in the thermal reservoir resonating with the charger's frequency. In Eq.~(\ref{equation-6}), the battery is isolated from the environment for avoiding the decay of the quantum coherence and the thermalization, while the charger interacts with the environment for effectively extracting the thermal energy. The initial state of the quantum battery is set to be the vacuum state, i.e., the Fock state with $n=0$. The state of the charger is initially set to be the coherent or thermal state.


For the system governed by the Hamiltonian (\ref{equation-5}), the analytical solution of the charging dynamics can be obtained by the Laplace transform for the Heisenberg equations, which is also given in the Appendix (which involves in solving the quartic algebraic equation with single variable). The coupling between the battery and the charger involves only the quadratic power of the ascending and descending operators. Furthermore, as long as the initial quantum state is the Gaussian state, the subsequent quantum state is still stayed in the Gaussian state~\cite{A.Serafini2017}.
\section{III.~Main results}
\subsection{A.~For the closed system}
\begin{figure}[tbph]
\centering\includegraphics[width=10 cm]{figure2.eps}
\caption{(Color online) The change of the physical quantities with time for the closed system. (a, c) demonstrate the dynamics of $E_{e}(t)$, $E_{i}(t)$ and $E_{1}(t)$, while (b, d) give the dynamics of $S(t)$, $R(t)$ and $C(t)$. The system contains only the rotating wave term for $k_{L}=k_{C}=0.7$ in (a) and (b) and only the counter-rotating wave term for $k_{L}=-k_{C}=-0.7$ in (c) and (d). The coherent state $\protect\hat{\varrho}_{1}(0)=\left\vert \protect\alpha \right\rangle\left\langle\protect\alpha\right\vert$ with $\protect\alpha=2$ is chosen as the initial state of the charger and $\omega _{2}=1.3\omega _{1}$. }
\label{figure2}
\end{figure}

Most recent research has discussed the situation in which the frequency of the battery resonates with that of the charger~\cite{Downing2023, Downing2024}. For example, in a study of two-level spin batteries, the charger's resonance with the two-level system is highlighted to ensure the maximum energy transfer, as mentioned in Ref.~\cite{FZhao2021}. Compared to the qubit battery, the continuous variable quantum battery discussed in our model exhibits four new features. The first feature is that the more ergotropy can be obtained when the frequency of the battery is larger than that of the charger $(\omega_{2}>\omega_{1})$. For our model, the maximum value of $E_{e}(t)$ is $4\omega_{1}$ for $\omega _{1}=\omega_{2}$ under only considering the rotating wave coupling. The interaction energy $E_{i}(t)$ keeps zero in the whole process of the evolution and the energy of the battery is entirely derived from the charger. Fig.~\ref{figure2}(a) demonstrates the evolution of the energies $E_{1}(t)$, $E_{e}(t)$ and $E_{i}(t)$ with time for $\omega_{2}=1.3\omega_{1}$ when only the rotating wave coupling is considered. For $\omega_{2}>\omega_{1}$, the battery can acquire more energy from the system. The extra energy is derived from the part of the reduction in the interaction energy. The evolution of the battery is faster than that of the charger. One photon from the charger cannot form one photon in the battery, resulting in a part of the interaction energy being stored in the battery. The larger the battery's frequency, more energy can be extracted from the system. But, this does not mean that the battery can obtain an infinite amount of energy if we increase $\omega _{2}$ continuously when the charger's frequency $\omega _{1}$ is fixed. In fact, there is an upper limit for $E_{e}(t)$. Specifically, the limit is about $7.84\hbar\omega _{1}$ at $\omega_{2}=10^{4}\omega _{1}$ for the specific parameters given in Fig.~\ref{figure2}(a). Furthermore, for the system only considering the rotating wave coupling, above $80\%$ of the maximum energy for the battery does not come from the charger but from the interaction energy when $\omega _{2}=6\omega _{1}$ is satisfied. The enhancement of charging power (i.e., the derivative of $E_{2}(t)$ with time) can also be realized by increasing $\omega _{2}$, since the increase of the coupling strengthes $|g|$ and $|G|$ enhances the energy transfer between the battery and the charger. For $\omega_{2}<\omega_{1}$, the battery's energy is less than $4\omega _{1}$. The evolution of the charger is faster than that of the battery, a portion of the charger's energy is stored as the interaction energy, so the energy of the battery will decrease.


Fig.~\ref{figure2}(b) demonstrates the evolution of $R(t)$, $S(t)$ and $C(t)$ with time only considering the rotating wave coupling. All of the energy stored in the battery can be fully transferred to the useful work due to $R(t)=1$. It means that the determinant of the covariance matrix of the Gaussian state $D=1$ and the quantum state of the battery is pure in the whole process of the evolution~\cite{J.W.Xu2016}. The quantum pure state cannot be transferred into the mixed state by the rotating wave coupling. The quantum coherence $C(t)$ oscillates with the frequency of $2\pi\omega_{1}/3.86$, which is synchronized with the oscillation of the ergotropy $E_{e}(t)$ in Fig.~\ref{figure2}(a). The entropy $S(t)=0$ shows that the entanglement between the charger and the battery is not a prerequisite for achieving a nonzero ergotropy. The dynamics of the ergotropy is determined by the quantum coherence, while the quantum entanglement plays no role under only considering the rotating wave coupling.


Fig.~\ref{figure2}(c) demonstrates the evolution of the energies $E_{1}(t)$, $E_{e}(t)$ and $E_{i}(t)$ with time only considering the anti-rotating wave coupling. It is found that the energy of the charger is always larger than $4\omega_{1}$ in this situation. The battery's energy comes entirely from the interaction, rather than from the charger. This indicates that the anti-rotating wave coupling inhibits energy exchange between the charger and the battery. Whether we can hypothesis that the charger is directly prepared to the vacuum state, making the battery continuously extract the energy from the interaction. However, this hypothesis does not apply to reality. When the initial energy of the charger is zero, the system evolves and the battery gains energy, but cannot obtain ergotropy. Fig.~\ref{figure2}(d) demonstrates the evolution of $R(t)$, $S(t)$ and $C(t)$ with time only considering the anti-rotating wave coupling. Eighty percent of the battery's energy can be extracted throughout the process due to $R(t)=0.8$. The entropy, $S(t)$, oscillates over time. The entropy describes the disorder of the subsystem and the degree of entanglement of the whole system. The presence of nonzero entropy is not conducive for the battery's energy to be transferred into useful work. The oscillation of $S(t)$ is synchronized with $C(t)$. A comparison of Fig.~\ref{figure2}(b) with Fig.~\ref{figure2}(d) shows that the oscillation of the ergotropy is consistent with that of the quantum coherence, but not with that of the quantum entanglement. The second feature that can be deduced is that the quantum coherence is more significant than the quantum entanglement for the ergotropy of the continuous variable quantum battery, irrespective of the coupling being anti-rotating or rotating wave.


For our model, if the battery is prepared in the thermal state, there is no ergotropy within the battery. The reason is that there is no quantum coherence for the thermal state. However, if the initial state of the charger is at the thermal state, it is possible to obtain nonzero ergotropy for the battery. The prerequisite is that the interaction between the charger and the battery must contain both the rotating and anti-rotating wave couplings. That is to say, if only the rotating or anti-rotating wave coupling is included in the interaction, there is no available work in the battery at the same condition. Consequently, there is still no quantum coherence, implying that the compression effect of the anti-rotating wave coupling is insufficient to produce the ergotropy even the quantum entanglement exists in the system~\cite{Barzanjeh2022}. As mentioned above, the dynamical behaviors for $S(t)$, $R(t)$ and $C(t)$ in Fig.~\ref{figure2}(b) and Fig.~\ref{figure2}(d) are very different. The different quantum states for the battery can be induced by the anti-rotating and rotating wave couplings for the same initial states and the same parameters of the system in the process of the evolution. If both of the anti-rotating and rotating wave couplings are considered, the quantum state of the battery will be the superposition of such different quantum states, and the quantum coherence will be nonzero, leading to the nonzero ergotropy~\cite{J.X.Liu2021}.


Fig.~\ref{figure3} gives the result of the thermal charging when both of the anti-rotating and rotating wave couplings are considered. Fig.~\ref{figure3}(a) demonstrates the evolution of the energies $E_{1}(t)$, $E_{e}(t)$ and $E_{i}(t)$ with time. It is shown that $E_{1}(t)$, $E_{e}(t)$ and $E_{i}(t)$ oscillate over time and exhibit the beat phenomenon. The minimum of $E_{1}(t)$ is larger than $4\omega _{1}$ for the thermal photon number $n_{p}=4$. The energy of the battery originates from the interaction energy rather than the charger. The interaction energy can be subdivided into two parts, which are related to the rotating and anti-rotating wave couplings. Correspondingly, there exist two characteristic frequencies in the system corresponding to the couplings. The competition between the two couplings leads to the beat phenomenon of the charging dynamics of the quantum battery. The oscillating frequency for the slow component is one half of the difference of the two frequencies (i.e., beat frequency), while the oscillating frequency of the fast component is one half of the sum of the two frequencies. The values for the difference and sum frequencies are about $4\pi\omega_{1}/17.63$ and $4\pi\omega_{1}/3.61$, respectively. Furthermore, the ergotropy $E_{e}(t)$ is nonzero for most of the evolution time. The nonzero ergotropy is related with the nonzero quantum coherence. Fig.~\ref{figure3}(b) demonstrates the evolution of $R(t)$ and $C(t)$ with time. Except for the same oscillating behavior, $R(t)$ is synchronized with $C(t)$. $R(t)$ and $C(t)$ are strongly correlated each other. The importance of the quantum coherence is further verified since the nonzero coherence leads to the nonzero ergotropy. Fig.~\ref{figure3} indicates that the utilization of the rotating wave coupling in conjunction with the anti-rotating wave coupling can yield an unexpected outcome, i.e., the ergotropy can be extracted for continuous variable quantum battery by the thermal charging. This is the third feature and most important result in the present paper. When combining the rotating and anti-rotating wave couplings, what value of $k_{C}$ and $k_{L}$ can be taken to ensure that the ergotropy is maximized for a given set of conditions?

\begin{figure}[tbph]
\centering\includegraphics[width=10 cm]{figure3.eps}
\caption{(Color online) (a) Dynamics of $E_{e}(t)$, $E_{i}(t)$ and $E_{1}(t)$; (b) Dynamics of $R(t)$ and $C(t)$. The thermal state with an average thermal photon number $n_{p}=4$ is chosen as the initial state of the charger. The other parameters are $\protect\omega _{2}=1.3\omega_{1}$, $k_{L}=-0.57$ and $k_{C}=0.7$. }
\label{figure3}
\end{figure}

Fig.~\ref{figure4} gives the change of Max$[E_{e}(t)]$ and Max$[R(t)]$ with $k_{C}$ and $k_{L}$. It is found that the larger values of Max$[E_{e}(t)]$ and Max$[R(t)]$ always satisfy the condition $k_{C}k_{L}<0$, which indicates that the strength of the anti-rotating coupling is greater than that of the rotating wave coupling (i.e., $|g+G|>|g-G|\geq0$). This can be deduced by means of reasoning based on the conditions of the rotating wave approximation. The anti-rotating wave coupling only plays key role when its strength is substantial, thereby significantly impacting the system's dynamical behaviors. To illustrate this, the relationship between the maximum values of $E_{e}(t)$ and $R(t)$ with $k_{C}$ and $k_{L}$ under condition of $n_{p}=4$ is demonstrated in Fig.~\ref{figure4}(a) and Fig.~\ref{figure4}(b), with the aim of providing a reference for the practical operation for the performance of the quantum batteries. Indeed, the greater the initial energy, the more power is used to drive the evolution. The maximum ergotropy is monotonically increased by enhancing the average number of photons for a specific evolution period, as shown in Fig.~\ref{figure5}(a). However, it should be noted that the relationship between the maximum value of $R(t)$ and the average number of photons $n_{p}$ over an evolution period cannot be determined by theoretical analysis. It can only be obtained through numerical calculation. Fig.~\ref{figure5}(b) shows that Max$[R(t)]$ with $n_{p}$ is a convex function. So, can the value of Max$[R(t)]$ saturate and approach to $1$? We predict that Max$[R(t)]\rightarrow1$ if $n_{p}\rightarrow+\infty$. In order to verify our prediction, we take $n_{p}=10^{5}$ in the numerical calculation, Max$[R(t)]=0.996$ is obtained. In the present paper, we do not study the situation of $|k_{C,L}|\geq1$, which would make the system to enter the region of deep strong coupling and lead to the energy divergence.

