\subsection{C.~Experimental scheme of our model}
The shape and structure of the two parallel plate capacitors are assumed to be identical. The plates are squares with the side length of $1$mm. The distance between the two plates of each capacitor is $90\mu m$. The relative positions of the two capacitors are shown in Fig.~\ref{figure1}. The four plates are located in the plane parallel to each other and the vacuum as the dielectric, there is an effect on the area of $1mm^{2}$. If the distance between the two capacitors in the nearest plate is $20\mu m$, we can obtain $k_{C}=-0.201$ according to Ref.~\cite{L.Huang2015}. The result ignores the edge effect of the capacitor. Of course, this approximation is reasonable from the given parameters. For the two coupled inductive coils, we only make an estimate that the number of coils turns is very small, assume that the radius of the coils are $1mm$ and they are coaxial, the nearest end of the two inductive coils are apart $50\mu m$ (only quite $5\%$ of coil radius). We can ignore the edge effect and consider that there is basically no leakage phenomenon, $k_{L}$ is close to 1. Max$[E_{e}(t)]=82.12\hbar\omega_{1}$ and Max$[R(t)]=0.892$ for $k_{L}=0.99$ by our numerical calculation. According to the analysis, it can be seen that the coupling between the capacitances $k_{C}$ is the core factor that determines the charging distance, and the wireless charging distance of the continuous variable quantum battery exceeds the charging distance of the current qubit battery. This is the last feature of our continuous variable quantum battery.


\section{IV.~Conclusions}
In this paper, the wireless and remote charging of a continuous variable quantum system based on the two coupled LC circuits is studied. The coupling between capacitance and inductance achieves the goal that the couplings in the Hamiltonian of the system contain only the rotating or anti-rotating wave. This paper points out that the rotating wave is helpful to transfer energy between the charger and the battery, while the anti-rotating wave coupling can transform the interacting energy into ergotropy. Enhancing the anti-rotating wave coupling results in more ergotropy. For the case that the initial quantum state of the charger is at the thermal state. The rotating or anti-rotating wave coupling alone cannot make the ergotropy exist in the battery. It is necessary to make the two couplings cooperate with each other and produce quantum coherence in the system. It provides an idea for the use of the energy of the thermal state or even the thermal reservoir. At the same time, we have simulated the effects of $k_{L}$ and $k_{C}$ on the maximum value of $E_{e}(t)$ and $R(t)$, which provides a numerical simulation for the actual use of the energy of the thermal state. The numerical simulations reveal that the presence of detuning between the battery and the charger can lead to more ergotropy for the battery. This conclusion is different from what previous studies predicted. In terms of precise manipulation, our proposed long-distance charging scheme has lower operational requirements and longer charging distances. Finally, a method that allows the strong coupling to contain only the rotating or counter-rotating wave is presented, which provides a solution to study the effect of containing only rotating or counter-rotating wave coupling in experiments.



