Several results collectively called the ``Vergleichsstellens\"{a}tze'' have been derived in \cite{fritz2022}. Of all of them, we will need the following version:

\begin{theorem}[Based on Theorem 8.6 in \cite{fritz2022}]\label{thm:Fritz2022}
Let $S$ be a zerosumfree preordered semidomain with a power universal element $u$. Assume that for some $d\in\N$ there is a surjective homomorphism $\|\cdot\|:S\to\R_{>0}^d\cup\{(0,\ldots,0)\}$ with trivial kernel and such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:surjectivehomomorphismproperties}
a\succeq b\ \Rightarrow\ \|a\|=\|b\|\quad {\rm and} \quad \|a\|=\|b\|\ \Rightarrow\ a\sim b.
\end{equation}
Denote the component homomorphisms of $\|\cdot\|$ by $\|\cdot\|_{(j)}$, $j=1,\ldots,d$. Let $x,y\in S\setminus\{0\}$ with $\|x\|=\|y\|$. If 
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] for every $\mb K\in\{\R_+,\R_+^{\rm op},\T\R_+,\T\R_+^{\rm op}\}$ and every nondegenerate monotone homomorphism $\Phi : S \to \mb K$ with trivial kernel, we have $\Phi(x) > \Phi(y)$, and
\item[(ii)] $\Delta(x) > \Delta(y)$ for every monotone $\|\cdot\|_{(j)}$-derivation $\Delta : S \to \R$ with $\Delta(u) = 1$ for all component indices $j = 1,\ldots,d$,
\end{itemize}
then
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] there exists a nonzero $a\in S$ such that $ax\succeq ay$, and 
\item[(b)] if additionally $x$ is power universal, then $x^n \succeq y^n$ for all sufficiently large $n\in\N$. 
\end{itemize}
Conversely if either of these properties holds for any $n$ or $a$, then the above inequalities hold non-strictly.
\end{theorem}

Large-sample ordering as in (a) in the above Theorem implies catalytic ordering as in (b), with a catalyst of the form $a=\sum_{\ell=0}^{n-1} x^\ell y^{n-1-\ell}$ for a sufficiently large $n\in\N$. This was shown in \cite{Duan2005} when $x,y$ are probability vectors, but the proof extends directly to the more abstract setting considered here. The converse implication typically does not holds; Theorem 3 of \cite{Feng_et_al_2006} provides a recipe for deriving counter examples in a particular setting.

 \subsection{Jordan's Lemma}

In our earlier classical results in \cite{farooq2024,verhagen2024}, we were aided by the fact that tuples of classical probability vectors can be jointly decomposed into single-entry tuples. This enabled us to utilize Theorem \ref{thm:Fritz2022} in a simple manner directly in the classical case in finding the monotone homomorphisms and derivations since these maps can now be decomposed into simpler maps on smaller spaces. This is naturally impossible in the quantum case. However, in some settings we may proceed in a way similar to the classical case. Namely, Jordan's Lemma \cite{Jordan1875} says that we may jointly decompose a pair of projections so that the components are of rank at most 1:

    %We would like to decompose density matrices in a direct sum of smaller matrices. Furthermore, we would like to have an upper bound on the dimension of these smaller matrices. Then, using the additivity of monotones, the problem of characterizing the test spectrum reduces to finding the value of the monotones on these smaller matrices. A single density matrix can be diagonalized, but in general, when considering a $d$-tuple of density matrices there is no way to decompose them all simultaneously in the same direct sum of smaller Hilbert spaces. However, in the case $d=2$, Jordan's Lemma offers some hope in the case of density matrices that are (proportional to) projectors. 
	\begin{lemma}[Jordan's Lemma]
		Let $\Hil$ be a finite-dimensional linear space and $A,B$ projections in $\Hil$ (i.e. two operators on $\Hil$ such that $A^2=A=A^*,B^2=B=B^*$). Then $A,B$ can be simultaneously block-diagonalized with blocks of dimension $1$ or $2$. In the case of dimension $2$, the corresponding blocks in $A,B$ have rank $1$. More precisely, there exists a decomposition $\Hil=\bigoplus_{i=1}^n\Hil_i$, for some $n\geq1$, where the $\Hil_i$ are at most $2$ dimensional linear spaces, such that 
		\begin{equation}\label{eq:Jordanform}
            A=\bigoplus_{i=1}^n A_i,\quad B=\bigoplus_{i=1}^n B_i, 
		\end{equation}
        where the $A_i$, $B_i$ are projections of rank at most $1$ in $\Hil_i$. 
	\end{lemma}

    Naturally also pairs of flat states may be similarly decomposed into at most rank-1 components to obtain a form like in \eqref{eq:Jordanform}, up to an overal normalization. This motivates us to construct a preordered semiring out of flat states where we can find all the monotone homomorphisms and derivations and derive Theorems \ref{thm:LS} and \ref{thm:rates} using Theorem \ref{thm:Fritz2022}.

    \section{Analyzing the semirings}\label{sec:technical}

    The preceding machinery has been successfully used previously in problems of classical matrix majorization in \cite{farooq2024,verhagen2024} and we will need some results derived also therein. In particular, we will assume the reader is familiar with the algebraic notions introduced in section 2.4 in \cite{verhagen2024}. 

    \subsection{Defining the semirings}
    We consider the set $V$ consisting of all pairs of self-adjoint matrices that are mutually block-diagonalizable into blocks of rank at most $1$. More precisely, $(A,B)\in V$ when $A,B$ are positive semi-definite operators on a Hilbert space
    \begin{equation}
        \Hil=\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\Hil_i, 
    \end{equation}
    where $\Hil_1,\ldots,\Hil_n$, $n\geq1$, are Hilbert spaces (which may all vary), such that $A,B$ admit a decomposition  
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:Vpair}
        A=\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\ketbra{\alpha_i}{\alpha_i},\quad B=\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}\ketbra{\beta_i}{\beta_i}, 
    \end{equation}
    where $\ket{\alpha_i},\ket{\beta_i}\in\Hil_i$ are vectors (possibly equal to the zero-vector). 

    We define the set of \emph{minimal restrictions} pairs $V_{\rm m.r.}\subsetneq V$ to be those $(A,B)\in V$ such that either $A$ and $B$ are both the $0$-operator in their associated Hilbert space, or $A$ and $B$ are at least partially overlapping in the sense that 
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:cscondition}
        \braket{\alpha_i}{\beta_i}\neq0\text{ for some }i\in[n]. 
    \end{equation}
    Also, we define the set of \emph{everywhere overlapping} pairs $V_{\rm e.o.}\subsetneq V_{\rm m.r.}$ to be those $(A,B)\in V_{\rm m.r.}$ such that, %$A$ and $B$ have the same support, i.e.
    for any $i\in[n]$, we have either 
    \begin{equation}
        \braket{\alpha_i}{\beta_i}\neq0 
    \end{equation}
    or 
    \begin{equation}
        \ket{\alpha_i}=0=\ket{\beta_i}. 
    \end{equation}
    Note that any pair of flat states or pair of pure states (that are at least partially overlapping) is in $V$. %Note also that the word `support' above is not really the support of the positive semi-definite operators involved, i.e.,\ the orthogonal complement of the kernel of the operator. To us, the `support' is the set of indices $i$ in the decomposition \eqref{eq:Vpair} where the vectors $\ket{\alpha_i}$ and $\ket{\beta_i}$ have a positive overlap.

    $V$ is closed under the $\boxplus$ and $\boxtimes$ operations, defined by 
    \begin{equation}
        (A,B)\boxplus(C,D):=(A\oplus C,B\oplus D),\quad(A,B)\boxtimes(C,D):=(A\otimes C,B\otimes D), 
    \end{equation}
    as one easily sees. We say that $(A,B)\in V$ with associated Hilbert space $\Hil$ majorizes $(C,D)\in V$ with associated Hilbert space $\Hil'$ and write $(A,B)\succeq(C,D)$ if and only if there exists a quantum channel $\Cha:\Lin(\Hil)\rightarrow\Lin(\Hil')$ such that $\Cha(A)=C$ and $\Cha(B)=D$. 

    We define the equivalence relation $\approx$ as follows: Let $(A,B),(A',B')\in V$ with associated Hilbert spaces $\Hil$, respectively $\Hil'$, and write $(A,B)\approx(A',B')$ if and only if they are equal up to a permutation of the Hilbert spaces $\Hil_i$, adding/removing copies of the zero-row $(0\,0)$, or by transformations of the form 
    \begin{equation}
        V_i\ketbra{a_i}{a_i}V_i^*=W_i\ketbra{a'_i}{a'_i}W_i^*,\quad V_i\ketbra{b_i}{b_i}V_i^*=W_i\ketbra{b'_i}{b'_i}W_i^*, 
    \end{equation}
    where the $V_i,W_i$ are isometries. %[Maybe this is equivalent to: there exist isometries $V\in\Lin(\Hil,\Hil_0),W\in\Lin(\Hil',\Hil_0)$ such that 
    %\begin{equation}
    %    VAV^*=WA'W^*,\quad VBV^*=WB'W^*.] 
    %\end{equation}
    For $(A,B)\in V$, we denote by $[(A,B)]$ the $\approx$-equivalence class of $(A,B)$. Define $S_{\rm m.r.}:=V_{\rm m.r.}/\approx$. The operations $\boxplus$ and $\boxtimes$ induce well-defined operations on $S$ that are now also commutative. Also, the preorder $\succeq$ induces a preorder on $S$. In conclusion, $(S_{\rm m.r.},\boxplus,0:=[(0\,0)],\boxtimes,1:=[(1\,1)],\succeq)$ is a preordered semiring. We may also define the preordered semiring based on $S_{\rm e.o.}=V_{\rm e.o.}/\!\approx$ in the same way. In the sequel, we will often treat elements of $V_{\rm m.r.}$ or $V_{\rm e.o.}$ as elements of the semirings $S_{\rm m.r.}$ or $S_{\rm e.o.}$, i.e.,\ we usually ignore the $\approx$-equivalence classes. We will also treat functions (such as the monotone homomorphisms) on the semirings as functions on $V_{\rm m.r.}$ or $V_{\rm e.o.}$; we simply assume that they are constant on each equivalence class. 

    In the rest of this paper, we will usually write a pair $(A,B)\in V$ as follows: 
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:Vpair2}
        A=\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}p_i\ketbra{\alpha_i}{\alpha_i},\quad B=\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}q_i\ketbra{\beta_i}{\beta_i}, 
    \end{equation}
    where the vectors $\ket{\alpha_i},\ket{\beta_i}$, $i=1,\ldots,n$, are assumed to be normalized, or equal to $0$, and $p_i,q_i\geq0$, $i=1,\ldots,n$. Note that when $(A,B)$ is a pair of quantum states, meaning that $\Tr[A]=1=\Tr[B]$, then $p_1+\ldots+p_n=1=q_1+\ldots+q_n$, i.e. the $p_i$ and the $q_i$ are both finite probability distributions. One sees that such pairs are precisely those in $\mc F$, i.e. cq-states with pure components that have some overlap. 

    We introduce some notation that will make the connection with the previously studied classical semirings in \cite{farooq2024,verhagen2024}, whose elements are written as two-column matrices, more clear. A pair $(A,B)\in V$ as in \eqref{eq:Vpair2} can be written as a matrix
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:matrixform}
        \left(\begin{array}{cc}
            p_1\ketbrar{\alpha_1}&q_1\ketbrar{\beta_1}\\
            \vdots&\vdots\\
            p_n\ketbrar{\alpha_n}&q_n\ketbrar{\beta_n}
        \end{array}\right). 
    \end{equation}
    Also, if we have $|\braket{\alpha_i}{\beta_i}|=1$ for some $i$, then we may regard this as a ``classical row'' of the above matrix and we simply omit the corresponding $\ketbrar{\alpha_i}$ and $\ketbrar{\beta_i}$ in the notation. 

    In \cite{verhagen2024}, Section 3, we discussed a semiring of $d$-tuples of vectors ($d\in\N$) with non-negative entries where the vectors needed to have some common support but they could also have partially disjoint supports. This semiring was there called the minimal restrictions semiring. Our current preordered semirings $S_{\rm m.r.}$ and $S_{\rm e.o.}$ both contain this semiring in the case $d=2$: it is the subset of those $(A,B)\in V_{\rm m.r.}$ where $A$ and $B$ commute. This means that we know something of the monotone homomorphisms of these semirings already based on our earlier work, and we will use this knowledge in our analysis of $S_{\rm m.r.}$ and $S_{\rm e.o.}$. %[Precisely which classical semirings are in which quantum semiring?]

    \subsection{Power universal elements}
    We will show which elements in the semirings $S_{\rm m.r.}$ and $S_{\rm e.o.}$ are power universals. Essentially they are precisely those with some quantum component. We first present a useful result due to Uhlmann \cite{Uhlmann1985}: 
    \begin{proposition}[Uhlmann]\label{propo:Uhlmann}
        Let $\ket{\alpha_1},\ket{\beta_1}\in\Hil_1$ and $\ket{\alpha_2},\ket{\beta_2}\in\Hil_2$ be normalized vectors in some Hilbert spaces $\Hil_1,\Hil_2$. Then 
        \begin{equation}
            (\,\ketbrar{\alpha_1},\ketbrar{\beta_1}\,)\succeq(\,\ketbrar{\alpha_2},\ketbrar{\beta_2}\,)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad|\braket{\alpha_1}{\beta_1}|\leq|\braket{\alpha_2}{\beta_2}|. 
        \end{equation}
    \end{proposition}

    Using the preceding proposition, we may prove a characterization for power universals first in $S_{\rm e.o.}$.

    \begin{proposition}\label{prop:puS}
    Let $(A,B)$ be a pair of (normalized) quantum states of the form \eqref{eq:Vpair2}. Then $(A,B)$ is a power universal of $S_{\rm e.o.}$ if and only if $0<|\braket{\alpha_i}{\beta_i}|<1$ for $i=1,\ldots,n$.
    \end{proposition}

    Let us note that the above condition for the power universals of $S_{\rm e.o.}$ essentially means that all the components of the power universals are, in a sense, purely quantum.

    \begin{proof}
    Let us first consider a simpler case where we show that a pair $(\ketbrar{\alpha},\ketbrar{\beta})$ of pure states such that $0<|\braket{\alpha}{\beta}|<1$ is a power universal of $S_{\rm e.o.}$. To this end, let us fix an element $(A',B')\in V_{\rm e.o.}$ such that $\tr[A']=1=\tr[B']$ where
    \begin{align}
    A'&=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n'}p'_j\ketbrar{\alpha'_j},\label{eq:a'}\\
    B'&=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n'}q'_j\ketbrar{\beta'_j}.\label{eq:b'}
    \end{align}
    Since we are within $V_{\rm e.o.}$, we may assume that $p'_j,q'_j>0$ for all $j=1,\ldots,n'$. We next show that, for this generic (normalized) element $(A',B')$, we have
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:apu1}
    (\ketbrar{\alpha},\ketbrar{\beta})^{\boxtimes m}\succeq (A',B')
    \end{equation}
