  \abstract 
   {
We performed deep X-ray observations of the Virgo cluster using the \textit{Einstein Probe} Follow-up X-ray Telescope (\textit{EP-FXT}) with a total exposure of 295 ks. 
Taking advantage of the large field of view (FoV) and low particle background of \textit{EP-FXT}, the image reveals a giant spiral feature connecting the cold fronts in the northwest and southeast, forming a coherent structure consistent with previous \textit{XMM-Newton} and \textit{Suzaku} findings.
Furthermore, we present two-dimensional maps of temperature, metallicity, and entropy in the Virgo Cluster, covering a FoV of approximately $28.5^{\prime}$. These maps clearly depict a spiral structure characterized by high density, low temperature, high metallicity, and low entropy.
These results support the scenario in which the spiral morphology originates from gas sloshing induced by a minor merger.
In addition, the temperatures measured with \textit{EP-FXT} show reasonable consistency with those obtained from \textit{XMM-Newton}, within uncertainties. }


   \keywords{EP-FXT -- X-ray -- galaxy cluster -- Virgo Cluster
               }

