\section{Imaging analysis}\label{sec:3}

To visualize faint structures in the image, we applied two different techniques for image enhancement.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{adpggm_70.png}
\caption{Adaptive GGM filtered image in the central Virgo cluster in 0.3-2.5 keV with SNR = 70. Prominent features are labeled.  The two dashed line to mark the giant spiral are identical to those in Fig.~\ref{fig:res}.}
\label{fig:ggm}
\end{figure}

Gradient filtering enhances the visibility of both sharp and flat image features, thereby facilitating their detection and analysis \citep{sanders2016very,sanders2016detecting,sanders2022studying}. 
Because of the high noise in X-ray image gradient measurements within low-brightness regions, the adaptive Gaussian Gradient Magnitude (GGM) filtering is employed for dynamic scale selection. This method dynamically adjusts filter parameters based on local gradients, enabling better capture of image textures and edges \citep{sanders2022studying}. Additionally, adjusting the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) helps smooth the image, enhances the SNR, and reveals finer details. Using these techniques, we applied the adaptive GGM filter to the vignetting-corrected and background-subtracted image in the 0.3–2.5 keV energy range, see Fig.~\ref{fig:ggm}.

The image clearly shows two X-ray arms, with the southwestern arm exhibiting a clockwise rotation toward the southeast \citep{forman2007filaments}. This rotational feature may be attributed to the interaction between relativistic jets from the AGN and the ICM \citep{forman2003reflections}. Furthermore, the image also identifies a cavity, a shock to the northwest, and cold fronts to the southeast and northwest. These features are consistent with the analysis by \citet{sanders2016detecting} using the \textit{Chandra} data. We also identified a region of significantly reduced surface brightness outward from the southwestern X-ray arm, which may indicate the presence of a cavity. 
The outskirts of M87 exhibit a prominent spiral morphology, extending from the northwest to the southeast, with similar spiral features also visible in the \textit{XMM-Newton} mosaic image \citep{roediger2011gas,simionescu2010metal}.
We then generate a residual image to better visualize this large-scale faint feature.

First, we need to estimate the first-order radial profile using the well-known double $\beta$ model and subtract it.  
As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:1d_profile}, the red solid line represents the best-fit double $\beta$ model to the \textit{EP-FXT} data (black points). 
The blue points represent the \textit{XMM-Newton} data and the green points represent the \textit{Chandra} data, for comparison.
As shown, apart from the central region affected by the AGN, the \textit{EP-FXT} data are in agreement with the double $\beta$ model and exhibit consistency with the radial profiles measured by the three telescopes. Furthermore, thanks to \textit{EP-FXT}'s large FoV, its observational range extends to regions up to $30^{\prime}$ from the outskirts of M87.

