The residual image reveals a few patterns, including X-ray arms and discontinuities which indicate cold fronts or shocks.
Notably, we identified a ring-like structure outside the X-ray arms, which displays a break in the southern part.
This may be attributed to interactions between the AGN jets and the ICM \citep{forman2003reflections}.
Most importantly, the large-scale spiral structure with excessive brightness is evident on the outskirts, spiraling from the cold front in the northwest to the southern cold gas clump, marked with blue dashed lines in Fig.~\ref{fig:res} and white dashed lines in Fig.~\ref{fig:ggm}. 
Consistent with the mosaic \textit{XMM-Newton} results \citep{simionescu2010metal}, the \textit{EP-FXT} observations clearly reveal the spiral features as continuous and well-defined across the entire FoV, likely owing to the instrument’s low particle background and wide spatial coverage.



The northwestern outer edge (a cold front) of the spiral lies approximately $18.3^{\prime}$ from the center. The structure rotates counterclockwise toward the south, with its southern inner edge located approximately $17.5^{\prime}$ from the center.

