\subsection{Giant sloshing spiral}


Based on the above analysis, we further confirmed the previously identified spiral structure using the GGM and residual images from \textit{EP-FXT}, which provide a comprehensive view of the feature.
Subsequently, we performed imaging-spectral analysis to construct two-dimensional maps of temperature, metallicity, and entropy on the outskirts of M87, providing a spatially resolved characterization of the thermodynamic properties of the spiral structure.
The results show that along the extent of the spiral, metallicity is enhanced, while temperature and entropy are reduced.
In addition, the spiral structure exhibits significant surface brightness enhancements, with cooler gas preferentially concentrated in the brighter regions, forming an alternating pattern of bright, cold areas and dimmer, hotter regions.
Numerical simulations by \citet{roediger2011gas} predict that such structures should exhibit low temperatures, high densities, and elevated metallicity.
Our observations are consistent with these predictions, supporting the scenario in which the large-scale spiral structure is produced by gas sloshing triggered by a minor merger event \citep{ascasibar2006origin}.


Figure~\ref{fig:res_img} shows the large spiral structure in Virgo, detected by \textit{EP-FXT}, consisting of an inner ring-like structure and outer spiral arms, which are delineated by a white spiral line.
In the northeast of the spiral structure (as indicated by the white arrow), the X-ray surface brightness is lower than in the surrounding regions, forming a concave feature. Two additional X-ray concave regions are also present in the west-southwest. 
This structure is similar to the Kelvin-Helmholtz rolls presented in the simulations and the smeared-out spiral structures observed in A496 \citep{walker2017there,ghizzardi2014metal}. This similarity may be attributed to the influence of the Kelvin-Helmholtz instability (KHI), which generates turbulence and disturbances near the cold front, potentially affecting the formation of the spiral structure.

Overall, \textit{EP-FXT} provides a clear and comprehensive view of the large-scale spiral pattern in M87. The spiral feature appears to rotate counterclockwise, extending from the cold front in the southeast to the cold front in the northwest, and further spiraling toward the cold gas clump in the southern outskirts. This spiral feature is associated with low temperature, low entropy, and high metallicity, consistent with expectations for gas sloshing phenomena. The prominent and well-defined morphology revealed by \textit{EP-FXT} offers valuable insights into the formation and evolution of spiral structures in galaxy clusters.


\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:6}

This paper provides a detailed analysis of the images and spectra of the Virgo cluster with \textit{EP-FXT}.
In the images, we present a prominent and complete large-scale spiral structure.
Furthermore, we produce two-dimensional maps of temperature, entropy, and metallicity that clearly illustrate the characteristics of the M87 spiral structure.
The main results of this work are summarized as follows:

\begin{enumerate} 

\item In the GGM image, multiple structures are identified. Two prominent X-ray arms are clearly visible: the outer side of the southwest arm bends toward the southeast, while the east arm bends toward the northwest. A shock from the northwest appears to connect to the east arm. Furthermore, cold fronts in the southeast and northwest are also readily discernible. These structures are broadly consistent with previous findings.


\item Based on the residual map (see Fig.~\ref{fig:res}), an ring-like structure with enhanced surface brightness is found in the outer X-ray arms. This ring-like structure, connected to the northwestern cold front, rotates counterclockwise, forming a large, outward-extending spiral structure. This structure exhibits low temperature, low entropy, and high density, which are characteristics consistent with gas sloshing induced by the merger of a gasless subcluster.


\item Temperatures measured with \textit{EP-FXT} are consistent with those obtained with \textit{XMM-Newton} within errors.

