Fig.~\ref{Mom_Fe_high_mom} depicts data obtained with a rotation of the sample that reduced the incidence angle to $\alpha=$\,\ang{37.6}. Simultaneously this moved the axis of the electron optics of the hemispherical detector to \ang{24.4} from the surface normal. In the figure the kinetic energy is fixed to 23.15\,eV, which is above the static Fermi level. The reduction in $\alpha$ leads to an decrease of the light intensity modulation wavelength $\Lambda$ and, consequently, to an increase of the observed oscillation frequency $\Omega$ as described in eqs. (\ref{Lambda_standing_Wave}) and (\ref{Oscillation_freq}), respectively. Thus more oscillations are visible in the same delay interval as in Figure~\ref{Mom_Fe_Gamma}.  On the other hand, at higher parallel momenta the oscillations visibly slow down as the perpendicular momenta decrease as given by Eq.~\ref{k_Pythagoras}. Again the complete data set can be fitted without systematic deviation by adjusting geometry and pulse parameters.\par\smallskip

