\section{Introduction}
\label{sec1}
%% Labels are used to cross-reference an item using \ref command.
We report a finding on hypothesis testing. An implication is that dynamical systems adapted to sequential inferential decisions (e.g. economies, markets, institutions) almost inevitably exhibit path-dependency, under which 'history matters', a key concern, as it increases complexity, fragility and inefficiency\textsuperscript
{\citenum{GTim}, \citenum{energy}, \citenum{hndbk}, \citenum{vol}}. Another is that inferential errors can be written as a sum of two independent terms, whose distinct properties are useful for error analysis and management.

%\section{Setup}\label{su}
%\subsection{Regular Hypothesis Testing}\label{gendef}
\section{Setup}
\subsection{Standard Sequential Testing}\label{st}
Consider the sequential testing of binary outcomes \(\{B=b\}\), \(b\in\mathcal{B}:=\{0,1\}\). Write the data process \(\{{D}^n\}\) \emph{cumulatively} so that data-to-date \(D^n:\Omega%\ni\omega
\mapsto\mathbf{V}^n%\ni D^n(\omega)
\) is a \(n\)-string of real-vectors \(D^n(i)(\omega)\in\mathbf{V}\), \(i=1,...,n\), on any sample-path \(\omega\in\Omega%\equiv\mathbf{V}^{\mathbb{N}}
\) of filtered space \((\Omega\equiv\mathbf{V}^{\mathbb{N}},\{\mathcal{F}_n\}, \mathcal{F}_{\infty}; {Q}_b)\) given the natural filtration \(\{\mathcal{F}_n\}\) of \(\{{D}^n\}\), with \(\mathcal{F}_\infty:=\sigma\big(\bigcup_{0}^{\infty}\mathcal{F}_n\big)\) supporting %\(B\)-labelled
some law \(Q_b\) of the data process when \(B=b\).

Most such tests use likelihood ratios (LR), optimal in the sense of Neyman-Pearson Lemma, %, with the objective law \(\mathbf{P}_B\) of data often unknown,  
based on some measure-pair, \(Q_b\) and \(Q_{\overline{b}}\), \(\overline{b}\not=b\in\mathcal{B}\). Given the data at any \(n\in\mathbb{N}\) and \(m<n\), with \(|_{(\cdot)}\) denoting restriction to \((\mathbf{V}^{(\cdot)},\mathcal{F}_{(\cdot)})\), define:\begin{equation}\label{lr}
L^{b\overline{b}}_n(\cdot):=\frac{Q_b|_n(\cdot)}{Q_{\overline{b}}|_{n}(\cdot)}\equiv\frac{Q_b|_{n}(\cdot|\mathcal{F}_{m})}{Q_{\overline{b}}|_{n}(\cdot|\mathcal{F}_{m})}\cdot\frac{Q_b|_{m}(\cdot)}{Q_{\overline{b}}|_{m}(\cdot)}=:L^{b\overline{b}}_{n|m}(\cdot|\mathcal{F}_{m})\cdot L^{b\overline{b}}_m(\cdot).
    \end{equation}Standard tests share the features below:
    \begin{enumerate}
    \item \label{simsim} \emph{Equivalence} \(Q_b|_{n}\sim Q_{\overline{b}}|_{n}\) holds on {any} partial-data space \((\mathbf{V}^{n}, \mathcal{F}_n)\), \(n\in\mathbb{N}\);        \item\label{regtest} Either \emph{mutual singularity} \(Q_b\perp Q_{\overline{b}}\), or equivalence \(Q_b\sim Q_{{\overline{b}}}\), holds on total-data space \((\mathbf{V}^{\mathbb{N}}, \mathcal{F}_\infty)\); call the former \emph{regular}, and the latter, \emph{non-resolving};
    \item
    \label{technical}The event 'balanced evidence' is a possibility at any finite stage of the test: there is a \emph{dense subset} of some open interval \(I\ni1\) on \(\mathbb{R}^+\) such that it is non-nil under the implied distribution of \emph{any} LR variable \(L^{b\overline{b}}_m\) or \(L^{b\overline{b}}_{n|m}\). 
    %implied (Lebesgue) probability mass of LR variable \(L^{b\overline{b}}_n\in\mathbb{R}^+\), as \(nto\infty\in\mathbb{N}\), is strictly positive on at least a dense subset of %at least one open interval containing \(1\).
\end{enumerate}
