    Many regular tests have data independently sampled from a distribution on \(\mathbb{R}\) (e.g. Normal), \(\mu_{b}\) or \(\mu_{\overline{b}}\). The limit measures on \(\mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{N}}\), \(\mu^{\mathbb{N}}_{b}\) and \(\mu^{\mathbb{N}}_{\overline{b}}\), are well-defined, and mutually singular unless \(\mu_{b}=\mu_{\overline{b}}\) (Kakutani's Theorem). For more general tests%under small and independent increments
    , to which Property-\ref{technical} applies asymptotically, see \ref{infps}%; inferential odds associated with these tests become log-Normal processes in continuous time
    .
