\subsection{The Resolution of Outcomes}
Regular tests resolves \(B\)-outcomes at \(T_{\mathcal{B}}=\infty\), almost surely (a.s.) under \(Q_B\):\begin{equation}
    \label{resolving}l^{b\overline{b}}_n:=\log L^{b\overline{b}}_n\to(-1)^{\mathbf{1}_{\{B=\overline{b}\}}}\cdot\infty,\text{ as }{n\to\infty},\end{equation}where the \emph{log-LR process} \(\{l^{b\overline{b}}_n\}\) best describes the dynamics (\ref{infps}). Non-resolving tests %, defined by measure-pairs that are equivalent on total data, \(Q_b\sim Q_{\overline{b}}\), 
    have \emph{convergent} log-LR processes (Radon-Nikodym Theorem).
