\section{Implications to Path-Dependency}
The true law \(\mathbf{P}_B\) of data %under \(\{B=\cdot\}\)
is often unknown, with the test measure-pair \(Q_B\) true up to equivalence: \(Q_B\sim\mathbf{P}_B\) and \(Q_B|_{n}\not=\mathbf{P}_B|_{n}\), \(\forall n\in\mathbb{N}\). This is adequate for \(B\)-detection, by the adjacency of inference \(\{\pi^b_n\}\) to its objectively true counterpart \(\{p^{{b}}_n\}\) under \(\{\mathcal{F}_n\}\) (Item-\ref{tails}, \ref{infps}). It does mean however \(\{\pi^b_n\}\not=\{p^b_n\}\) in general, and not just due to \(\pi^b_0\not=p^b_0\). Then, by Lemma \ref{l1}, decisions of the form \(\{X_n\}:=\{u({\pi^b_n})\}\) must be path-dependent against any state-variable of the form \(%\{y_n\}:=\{y_n\}-\{X_n\}\), \(
\{Y_n\}:=\{v(p^b_n)\}\), where \(u\) and \(v\) are continuous, with \(\{Y_n\}\) tracking some dynamical conditions, so that \(\{Z_n\}:=\{Y_n\}-\{X_n\}\) (or equivalent) characterises the system concerned.

Further, replacing \(\{p^{b}_n\}\) with any alternative inference \(\{\hat{\pi}^{b}_n\}\) adjacent but not redundant to \(\{{\pi}^{b}_n\}\), then Lemma \ref{l1} means that dynamics adapted to 'difference of opinions' between two participant groups (e.g. voters, buyers/sellers), and so to some state-variable like \(\{Z_n\}\), must appear path-dependent (to all participants).

Finally, %irrespective of details notwithstanding (e.g. trading mechanisms, trader preferences), %basket \(\mathcal{I}\) of
asset-pricing under risky-outcome \(B\) (e.g. expansion \emph{vs} recession), given maturity \(T<T_{\mathcal{B}}\) and \emph{\(B\)-sure} asset-price \(\{S^b_n\}\) {independent} of \(B\)-informative filtration \(\{\mathcal{F}_n\}\), has the form\textsuperscript
{\citenum{coh}} \(X_n= %\sum_{i\in \mathcal{I}}h^{(i)}_n(B=b_i)=
%Sigma_{b\in\mathcal{B}}\text{ }
u(\pi^b_n;S^b_n) %\Sigma_{i\in\mathcal{I}}\langle Y \rangle_{\pi_n}v(\pi^b_n))
\), \(n<T\), with \(u\) implementing (continuous) discounts to \(v(\pi^b_n):=\Sigma_{b\in\mathcal{B}}\text{ }\pi^b_nS^b_n\), the {expected asset-worth} given the \(\{\mathcal{F}_n\}\)-adapted \(B\)-belief \(\pi^b_n\). Then variable \(\{Z_n\}=\{Y_n\}-\{X_n\}\), where \(\{Y_n\}=\{v(p^{b}_n)\}\) is the realised asset-worth on average, tracks \emph{realised risk-premia}%\sum_{i\in\mathcal{I}}\langle Y^i \rangle_{p_n}\Sigma_{b\in\mathcal{B}}\text{ }p^b_ny_b\)
. As risk-compensation, a matter of economic decision, it is path-independent in usual theories, which take perfect \(B\)-sure knowledge, \(Q_B=\mathbf{P}_B\), for granted. When this fails, path-dependency in realised asset-prices and risk-premia follows, given Lemma \ref{l1}.
