\section{Implications to Inferential Errors}

Consider the errors in a decision process of the form \(\{u(p^b_n)\}-\{u(\pi^b_n)\}\), where \(u\) is \emph{analytic}, so that the object of interest is effectively \(\{Err_n\}:=\{p^b_n\}-\{\pi^b_n\}\). A typical problem of this form is the ongoing evaluation of an insurance policy based on the belief of \(\pi^b_0\) in the relevant hazard rate.

There are two components to \(\{Err_n\}\): with \(\sigma^{(\cdot)}_n:=\sqrt{(\cdot)^b_n(\cdot)^{\overline{b}}_n}\) at any \(n\in\mathbb{N}\),\begin{align}\label{total}
    &Err_n\equiv \big(\check{p}^b_n-\pi^b_n\big)+\big(p^b_n-\check{p}^b_n\big),\\
    \label{typ1}&%p^b_n-
    \check{p}^b_n-\pi^b_n
    =(\rho^{\frac{1}{2}}-\rho^{-\frac{1}{2}})%\sigma^p_n
    \sigma^{\check{p}}_n\sigma^\pi_n
    =\frac{\rho^{\frac{1}{2}}-\rho^{-\frac{1}{2}}}{\rho^{\frac{1}{2}}\pi^b_n+\rho^{-\frac{1}{2}}\pi^{\overline{b}}_n}(\sigma^\pi_n)^2,
\end{align}where \(\rho:=O[\check{p}^b_0]/O[\pi^b_0]\), and \(\{\check{p}^b_n\}\), with \(\check{p}^b_0=p^b_0%\pi^b_0
\), is the would-be inference process under the objectively true \emph{a priori} probability \(p^b_0\).%given knowledge of the objective \(B\)-sure laws.

Would-be inference \(\{\check{p}^b_n\}\) is informationally redundant to \(\{\pi^b_n\}\). %\(\{p^b_n\}\)
The first error term (\ref{typ1}) as a result is a path-independent bias (to the agent), with a \emph{constant sign}%(\(sign[\rho-1]\))
. For \(\sigma^{{\pi}}_0\ll\frac{1}{2}\) and \(\sigma^p_0\ll\frac{1}{2}\) (e.g. rare hazards), the level of \(\rho\) measures the degree of bias, with \(\rho>1\) (\(<1\)) indicating a bias for (against) the status quo.

The second error term, the difference process \(\{p^b_n\}-\{\check{p}^b_n\}\), is diffusive and path-dependent, adapted to \(B\)-informative filtration \(\{\mathcal{F}_n\}\). It is determined by the flaws in one's \(B\)-sure knowledge (i.e. \(\mathbf{P}_B-Q_B\)), and as such, independent in nature and behaviour to the first error term. It is easily and routinely modelled for tests with small and independent increments in continuous time (see (\ref{w}-\ref{w'})). Denote the log-LR process underpinning \(\{\check{p}^b_t\}\) (and so \(\{{\pi}^b_t\}\)) by \(\{{l}^{b\overline{b}}_t\}\), and the unknown objective log-LR process underpinning \(\{{p}^b_t\}\) by \(\{\mathbf{l}^{b\overline{b}}_{t}\}\). They are each associated with a signal-to-noise process, say \(\{\sigma^l_t\}\) and \(\{{\sigma}^\mathbf{l}_t\}\) respectively (as in (\ref{w'})), so that error \(\{p^b_n\}-\{\check{p}^b_n\}\) is governed essentially by \(\int^t_0 [(\sigma^{\mathbf{l}}_s)^2-({\sigma}^{l}_s)^2]ds\). It may grow systematically and so have an eventually almost-sure sign, with positivity (negativity) meaning a systematic under-reaction (overreaction) to data.

Thus the two error terms above may combine to mitigate or exacerbate total inferential error: when the fixed-signed pure-bias component favours or disfavours the status quo, systematic overreactions or under-reactions respectively (to data) can mitigate total error \(\{Err_n\}\) (see (\ref{total})), and vice versa.

\begin{remark}
    %Circumstances may dictate what the 'optimal allocation' of total error is with respect to error-types\footnote{Commerical concerns may favour a deliberate status quo bias at the outset, with the resulting locked-in errors thereafter mitigated through a maintenance strategy.}. Further,If an outcome is rare, one's \(B\)-sure knowledge can be quite accurate (given abundant 'high-frequency' samples) relative to that about low-frequency outcomes and so total error tends to be dominated by a fixed-signed path-independent bias.
    Circumstances may be such that it is known that the diffusive error component 'leans' towards overreaction and yet it is unclear how to modify one's \(B\)-sure knowledge to correct for it consistently, then behaving with an apparent bias for the status quo may be a simple and advantageous strategy.   
\end{remark}
