\section{Generalized tensor nilpotence for morphisms}

Let $\bC$ be an abelian monoidal category. Assume that the monoidal product is right exact. Let $f: A \to B$ in $\bC$. Denote 
$$f(n):=\{f \otimes \id_{A_1} \otimes f \otimes \id_{A_2} \otimes... \otimes \id_{A_{n-1}} \otimes f : \; \forall \; \text{ flat }A_i \in \bC\}.$$
We say that $f$ is a {\em{generalized nilpotent}} morphism if every morphism in $f(n)$ is the 0 morphism. 

We prove a noncommutative analogue of \cite[Lemma 4.18]{BKS2019}. 

\ble{serre-gen-nilp}
Let $f: A \to B$ be a morphism in $\bC$, and suppose that $A$ is central (there exists a natural isomorphism $A \otimes C \cong C \otimes A$ for all $C \in \bC$) and $B$ is flat (the functors $B \otimes -$ and $- \otimes B$ are exact). Then the collection of objects
\[
\Nil^l(f):=\{C \in \bC : \; \exists\; n \text{ with }\id_C \otimes g = 0 \; \forall \; g \in f(n)\}
\]
is a left Serre ideal of $\bC$. Likewise, the collection of objects
\[
\Nil^r(f):=\{D \in \bC : \; \exists\; n \text{ with }g \otimes \id_D = 0 \; \forall \; g \in f(n)\}
\]
is a right Serre ideal of $\bC$.
\ele

\begin{proof}
Since the proofs are the left and right sides are analogous, we prove the claim for $\Nil^l(f)$. It is clear that $\Nil^l(f)$ is closed under tensoring with arbitrary objects on the left. To show that $\Nil^l(f)$ is a Serre ideal, then, it suffices to show that it is closed under subobjects, quotients, and extensions. Suppose 
\[
0 \to C \xrightarrow{c} D \xrightarrow{d} E \to 0
\]
is a short exact sequence with $D \in \Nil^l(f)$, with $n$ such that for all $g \in f(n)$, we have $1_D \otimes g = 0$. Suppose $g: A \otimes A_1 \otimes A \otimes... \otimes A_{n-1} \otimes A \to B \otimes A_1 \otimes B \otimes... \otimes A_{n-1} \otimes B$ is a morphism in $f(n)$; we will denote the source and target of $g$ by $\tilde A$ and $\hat A$, for the sake of space. Note that since $B$, as well as each $A_i$, are flat, so is $\hat A$.

We now have a commutative diagram of exact rows
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
            & C \otimes \tilde A \arrow[r, "c \otimes 1_{\tilde A}"] \arrow[d, "1_C \otimes g"] & D \otimes \tilde A \arrow[r, "d \otimes 1_{\tilde A}"] \arrow[d, "1_D \otimes g"] & E \otimes \tilde A \arrow[r] \arrow[d, "1_E \otimes g"] & 0 \\
0 \arrow[r] & C \otimes \hat A \arrow[r, "c \otimes 1_{\hat A}"]                                & D \otimes \hat A \arrow[r, "d \otimes 1_{\hat A}"]                                & E \otimes \hat A \arrow[r]                              & 0
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}
The middle morphism $1_D \otimes g$ is equal to 0, by assumption. It immediately follows that $1_C \otimes g$ and $1_E \otimes g $ are also 0, and so $C$ and $E$ are in $\Nil^l(f)$, and so $\Nil^l(f)$ is closed under subobjects and quotients.

To show that $\Nil^l(f)$ is closed under extensions, suppose again that 
\[
0 \to C \xrightarrow{c} D \xrightarrow{d} E \to 0
\]
is exact, but now with $C$ and $E$ in $\Nil^l(f)$. We have $n_1$ and $n_2$ such that $1_C \otimes g = 0$ for all $g \in f(n_1)$, and $1_E \otimes h = 0$ for all $h \in f(n_2)$; pick $n$ as the maximum of $n_1$ and $n_2$, so that $1_C \otimes g = 1_E \otimes g = 0$ for all $g \in f(n)$. We will show that $1_D \otimes g \otimes g = 0$, which implies that $1_D \otimes h =0$ for any $h \in f(2n)$. As before, we will denote the source and target of $g$ by $\tilde A$ and $\hat A$. By the universal properties of kernels and cokernels respectively we obtain morphisms $a: D \otimes \tilde A \to C \otimes \hat A$ and $b: E \otimes \tilde A \to D \otimes \hat A$ such that $(c \otimes 1_{\hat A}) \circ a = 1_D \otimes g$ and $b \circ (d \otimes 1_{\tilde A}) = 1_D \otimes g$, as in the diagram
 \begin{center}
\begin{tikzcd}
            & C \otimes \tilde A \arrow[r, "c \otimes 1_{\tilde A}"] \arrow[d, "0"] & D \otimes \tilde A \arrow[r, "d \otimes 1_{\tilde A}"] \arrow[d, "1_D \otimes g"] \arrow[ld, "a"] & E \otimes \tilde A \arrow[r] \arrow[d, "0"] \arrow[ld, "b"] & 0 \\
0 \arrow[r] & C \otimes \hat A \arrow[r, "c \otimes 1_{\hat A}"]                    & D \otimes \hat A \arrow[r, "d \otimes 1_{\hat A}"]                                                & E \otimes \hat A \arrow[r]                                  & 0
\end{tikzcd}
\end{center}



\end{proof}
