\begin{abstract}
We give aperiodic tile sets based on Sturmian words of quadratic slopes. 
The method works for any quadratic irrational slope, and 
we can produce infinitely many aperiodic tile sets 
whose underlying scaling constant is a unit of 
any real quadratic field.
There are two key ingredients in our construction. 
The first one is ``Sturmian lattices''; 
an interesting grid structure generated by Sturmian words that 
emerged in an aperiodic monotile called Smith Turtle found in 
\cite{SMKGS:23_1}.
The second is the bounded displacement equivalence of Delone sets, 
which plays a central role in this construction.
\end{abstract}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
\label{Introduction}
Smith Turtle found in \cite{SMKGS:23_1} is an aperiodic monotile, i.e., it tiles the plane but only in non-periodic ways. 
There are several proofs of this fact and our starting point is a proof
in \cite{Akiyama-Araki:23}.
We draw the Ammann bars on the tile as in Figure~\ref{fig:SL}.

\begin{figure}[ht]\centering
\includegraphics[pagebox = artbox, 
width = .5\linewidth, page = 9]
{Example3.pdf}
\caption{Ammann bars on Smith Turtle\label{fig:SL}}
\end{figure}

Assuming a patch extends to a tiling, 
the bars must continue across the edge and form a
straight line, enforcing a lattice-like structure as in Figure~\ref{SL0}. This structure may look simple, but it is not. 

\begin{figure}[ht]\centering
\includegraphics[%pagebox = artbox, 
width = .8\linewidth, page = 1]
{Turtle.pdf}
\caption{Sturmian lattice}
\label{SL0}
\end{figure}

Whenever two lines meet, a third line of the remaining slope passes close by forming a tiny regular triangle.
Fixing one line, 
the tiny triangles appear above or below this line
without an obvious rule.
In this paper, we define the Sturmian lattice by 
generalizing this configuration and give a classification in \S \ref{SL}
in Theorems~\ref{Main0}~and~\ref{thm:rho-characterization}.
We shall see that these Sturmian lattices already enforce the
structure of the Sturmian words but include rational slopes.
The proof in \cite{Akiyama-Araki:23} is based on the fact that
that tile and its flipped version carry the Ammann bars of length $3:1$, and flipped tiles are in one-to-one correspondence to the tiny triangles that
appeared in the crossings. Indeed, 
this already implies that the slope corresponding to the
Sturmian lattice must be irrational, implying that the resulting tiling cannot be periodic.
Further, we see that adjacent gaps of 
the parallel Ammann bars correspond to the Sturmian sequence of 
slope $(-1 + \sqrt{5})/2$ or $(3 - \sqrt{5})/2$ in Corollary \ref{Sturm}.

In this paper, we wish to generalize this idea to an arbitrary
Sturmian lattice. 
Our main Theorem~\ref{thm:Voronoi-aperiodic} in \S \ref{Voronoi} reads
\medskip

{\it
For the Sturmian lattice of any quadratic irrational slope, 
we can construct an aperiodic tile set; a finite set of tiles
that can tile the plane but only in non-periodic ways.}
\medskip

A Sturmian word of a quadratic slope $\alpha$
is generated by a certain 
substitution whose expansion constant 
is a unit of a quadratic field $\Q(\alpha)$, see 
around Corollary~\ref{cor:superSL-2D}.
Therefore the corresponding
Sturmian lattice has a self-similar structure to this quadratic
unit expansion. 
It is remarkable that 
Theorem~\ref{thm:Voronoi-aperiodic} provides infinitely many essentially 
different aperiodic tile sets, since their
underlying expansion constants are the units 
in arbitrarily chosen real quadratic fields.

A common historical way to show the
aperiodicity of a given tile set 
is to show that it possesses hierarchical unique composition property,
but the proof usually becomes intricate and often requires computer assistance. Here the proof of aperiodicity is mathematically pretty simple. 
Everything boils down to a simple statement:
\medskip

{\it 
A quadratic curve and a line intersect at most two points.}
\medskip

It is also noteworthy 
that we do not use the self-similarity of Sturmian lattice in the 
proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:Voronoi-aperiodic}. 
As a consequence, 
the aperiodic tile set does not necessarily
enforce self-similarity of the resulting tiling. Indeed, 
we construct an example that the associated tiling space has positive 
topological entropy,
see \S\ref{sec:ex1}.

There is a downside to this construction. 
Because our construction heavily relies
 on the bounded displacement equivalence among Delone sets,
it does not provide a direct practical method of construction.
Consequently, we do not know a 
uniform bound on the number of tiles with respect to the choices of slopes.
Also, the tiles may not be topological disks, even worse, 
they could be disconnected in general.

Regardless of this deficiency, in \S \ref{Ex}, 
we give several concrete examples by making explicit the
bounded displacement equivalence using the self-similar structure of 
Sturmian lattice. 
We find that there is an aperiodic tile set 
consisting of 3 tiles; all of them are homeomorphic to a ball, and
one of its resulting tilings is self-similar 
with an expansion constant $\sqrt{2}-1$.
The construction of aperiodic tile sets using self-similarity seems handy, 
but we are not sure if this way works or not 
for the general quadratic slopes. 

\section{Aperiodic tile set}
We consider the Euclidean plane $\R^2$. Let $F$ be a finite set. 
A \emph{tile} is a pair $\T=(T,a)$ where $T\subset\R^2$  
% is a non empty compact set in $\R^2$
is a non-empty compact set in $\R^2$
which coincides with the closure of its interior
and $a\in F$. 
Note that it is important not to 
assume any further topological property of $T$ in this paper.
We say that $T$ is the \emph{support} of $\T$ 
and $a$ is the \emph{color} of $\T$.
%, and write $\supp(\T)=T$ and $\ell(\T)=a$.
A \emph{prototile set} $\AA=\{\T_1,\dots \T_m\}$ is a finite set of tiles
with $\T_i=(T_i,a_i)$.
%Let $G$ be a subgroup of $\mathrm{Isom}(\R^2)$
%which contain translations $x\mapsto x+u$ for all
%$u\in \R^2$.
An isometry $g\in \mathrm{Isom}(\R^2)$ acts on $\T=(T,a)$ by
$
g((T,a))=(g(T),a).
$
A collection 
$\P=\{ g_{i,j}(\T_i)\mid g_{i,j}\in J_i, \ i\in \{1,2,\dots,m\} \}$
with $J_i\subset \mathrm{Isom}(\R^2)$
is called a \emph{patch} if $g_{i,j}(T_i)$'s have disjoint interiors.
The \emph{support} of a patch $\P$ is defined by
\[
\supp(\P)=\bigcup_{i=1}^m \bigcup_{g_{i,j}\in J_i} g_{i,j}(T_i).
\]
The color is often used to distinguish tiles having congruent supports.
A \emph{translation} of a patch $\P$ by a vector $u \in \R^2$ is defined as
$$
\P+u=\{ (T+u,a)\mid (T,a)\in \P \}
$$
where $T+u=\{x+u\mid x\in T\}$.
A \emph{tiling} by $\AA$ is the patch that $\supp(\P)=\R^2$ 
and we use symbol $\TT$
for a tiling. 
This definition of tiles and 
tilings is standard and found in \cite[Definition 5.2]{BG}, 
which generalize the ones in \cite{GS}.
We say that $\AA$ \emph{admits a tiling} if there exists a tiling $\TT$
by $\AA$.
An element of 
$\Per(\TT)=\{u\in \R^2\mid \TT+u=\TT\}$ is called a \emph{period}. A tiling
is \emph{non-periodic} if $\Per(\TT)=\{0\}$ holds.
A prototile set $\AA$ is called \emph{aperiodic} if $\AA$ admits a tiling but all tilings by $\AA$ are non-periodic.
A tiling has \emph{finite local complexity} (FLC for short) 
for any $R>0$, there exists $n\in \N$ that 
the number of patches whose support is contained in the ball $B(x,R)$ centered at any $x\in \R^2$ 
does not exceeds $n$ up to isometry\footnote{Note that FLC is often defined with respect to translations, a subgroup of $\mathrm{Isom}(\R^2)$.}.
We also use a notion of \emph{patch-tile}. 
Given a tiling, if we can partition it into a finite set of patches up to rigid motion that each patch is understood as a new tile, then we call it a 
patch-tile, see \cite{Akiyama-Araki:23}. 

The prototiles often have markers or decorations, 
which restrict the adjacency of prototiles. 
In this paper, our prototiles are polygons or a union of polygons. 
The marker is 
given by some segments written on the ``surface'' of prototiles. 
We assume a matching rule that 
the segment continues across the edges of prototiles
and form a line. This matching rule is introduced by R.~Ammann 
and called Ammann bars (see \cite[Chapter 10.4]{GS},\cite{AGS:92}), 
as we saw in Figures~\ref{fig:SL}~and~\ref{SL0}.
Note that Smith Turtle is particularly special: 
the Ammann bars are dispensable.
We can show that there is no tiling which does not obey this rule, see \cite
[Lemma 2]{Akiyama-Araki:23}. 

\section{Sturmian Lattices}
\label{SL}

In the $(x,y)$ plane, we use a redundant triangular coordinate system $(a,b,c)$ as in Figure~\ref{TC}.

\begin{figure}[ht]\centering
\includegraphics[width=8cm]{triplets_coordinate.pdf}
\caption{Triangular Coordinates\label{TC}}
\end{figure}

This point $(a,b,c)$ is defined as the intersection point of the three lines 
$y=a$, $y=-\sqrt{3}x-2b$ and $y=\sqrt{3}x-2c$ with $a+b+c=0$.
The point $(a,b,c)\in (1/2)\Z^3$ gives a triangular lattice tiled by 
regular triangles of 
height $1/2$ and side length $1/\sqrt{3}$. 
We refer to the regular triangles of this size as ``tiny triangles'' hereafter.

\begin{definition}\label{def:SL}
A \emph{Sturmian lattice} is defined as a collection
\[
\mathfrak{A} \cup \mathfrak{B} \cup \mathfrak{C}:=
\{ a= a(i) \mid i\in \Z \} \cup \{ b = b(j) \mid j \in \Z \} \cup
\{ c = c(k) \mid k\in \Z \}
\]
of infinitely many lines in the plane determined 
by three functions $a, b, c\colon \Z\to \R$ with
\begin{equation}
\label{One}
a(i+1)-a(i) \ge 1,\quad b(j+1)-b(j) \ge 1,\quad c(k+1)-c(k) \ge 1
\end{equation}
% so that there exists a non-empty set $V\subset \Z^3$ with the following properties:
such that there exists a non-empty set $V \subset \Z^3$ satisfying the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\renewcommand{\labelenumi}{(SL-\arabic{enumi})}
\setcounter{enumi}{-1}
\item
$(0, 0, 0)\in V$.
\item
\begin{itemize}
\item
$\forall j, k\in \Z$, $\exists ! i\in \Z$ such that $(i, j, k)\in V$.
\item
$\forall i, k\in \Z$, $\exists ! j\in \Z$ such that $(i, j, k)\in V$,
\item
$\forall i, j\in \Z$, $\exists ! k\in \Z$ such that $(i, j, k)\in V$.
\end{itemize}
\item
For any $(i, j, k)\in V$, \[
\bigl|a(i) + b(j) + c(k)\bigr| = \frac{1}{2}.
\]
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{rem}
The condition~(SL-1) is automatically satisfied when 
every adjacent gap in \eqref{One} strictly exceeds 1.
\end{rem}

An element $(i, j, k)\in V$ gives a tiny triangle:
\[
a=a(i),\ b=b(j),\ c=c(k).
\]
Conditions (SL-1) and (SL-2) guarantee that, given two directions---say $a$ and $b$---the intersection point of the lines $a = a(i)$ and $b = b(j)$ determines a unique tiny triangle corresponding to an element of $V$.
In other words, (SL-1) and (SL-2) induce a bijection from $\Z\times \Z$ to $V$.

\begin{ex}
\label{ex:Kagome}
\begin{align*}
a(i)&= \frac{1}{2} + i,&
b(j)&= j,&
c(k)&= k
\end{align*}
yields the Kagome lattice in Figure~\ref{fig:KL}.

\begin{figure}[ht]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.7\linewidth]{Kagome_lattice.pdf}
\caption{Kagome Lattice\label{fig:KL}}
\end{figure}

\end{ex}

\begin{ex}
\label{ex:periodic}
For $r\in \Z$ and $i, j, k \in \{0, 1, 2\}$, setting
\begin{align*}
a(3r - i)&= \frac{1}{2} + 7r - 2i,&
b(3r + j)&= 7r + 2j,&
c(3r + k)&= 7r + 2k
\end{align*}
yields a Sturmian lattice in Figure~\ref{fig:PSL}, which is periodic in each of the three directions.
Indeed, one can directly verify that
\[
a(3r - i) + b(3r' + j) + c(3r'' + k) =
\frac{1}{2} + 7(r + r' + r'') + 2(-i + j + k)
\]
is equal to either $\pm1/2$ if and only if
\[
7(r + r' + r'') + 2(-i + j + k)\in \{0, -1\}
\]
holds.
Moreover, by $-i + j + k\in [-2, 4]\cap \Z$ we have
\begin{align*}
7(r + r' + r'') + 2(-i + j + k) = \phantom{+}0&\iff
(r + r' + r'', -i + j + k) = (0, 0),\\
7(r + r' + r'') + 2(-i + j + k) = -1&\iff
(r + r' + r'', -i + j + k) = (-1, 3).
\end{align*}
In particular, if we take
\[
V = \left\{(3r - i, 3r' + j, 3r'' + k)~\middle|~ \bigl|a(3r-i) + b(3r'+j) + c(3r''+k)\bigr| = \frac{1}{2}\right\},
\]
% then $V$ satisfies (SL-2), and
then $V$ satisfies (SL-2). Moreover,
\[
V\subset \bigl\{(3r-i, 3r'+j, 3r''+k)\bigm| (3r-i) + (3r'+j) + (3r''+k) = 0\bigr\}
\]
implies (SL-0) and (SL-1).
% Actually, these two sets are equal as we will see in Proposition~\ref{prop:characterization-V}.
In fact, these two sets are equal as we will see in Proposition~\ref{prop:characterization-V}.

\begin{figure}[ht]\centering
\includegraphics[width=.7\linewidth]{KAB_rational.pdf}
\caption{Example of a periodic Sturmian lattice}
\label{fig:PSL}
\end{figure}
\end{ex}

We begin with a characterization of $V$.

\begin{prop}
\label{prop:characterization-V}
For any integers $i,j,k$,
\[
(i,j,k)\in V \iff i+j+k=0.
\]
\end{prop}



\begin{proof}
Assume $(i, j', k), (i+1, j'', k)\in V$, i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:prop1-assumption}
\bigl|a(i) + b(j') + c(k)\bigr| =
\bigl|a(i+1) + b(j'') + c(k)\bigr| = \frac{1}{2}.
\end{equation}
We claim that $j'' = j' - 1$.
First notice that $j' = j''$ is impossible since $(i, j', k), (i+1, j', k)\in V$ breaks the axiom (SL-1).
%
In the case $j' < j''$, we have \[
b(j'') - b(j') =
\sum_{j = j'}^{j''-1}
\bigl(b(j + 1) - b(j)\bigr)\ge
\sum_{j = j'}^{j''-1}1 =
j' - j\ge 1
\]by \eqref{One}, and hence \[
\bigl(a(i + 1) - a(i)\bigr) +
\bigl(b(j'') - b(j')\bigr)\ge 2.
\]However, our assumption~\eqref{eq:prop1-assumption}
\begin{align*}
-\frac{1}{2}\le a(i + 1) + b(j'') + c(k)&\le \frac{1}{2},&
-\frac{1}{2}\le a(i) + b(j') + c(k)&\le \frac{1}{2}
\end{align*}
shows that \[
-1\le \bigl(a(i + 1) - a(i)\bigr) +
\bigl(b(j'') - b(j')\bigr)\le 1,
\]which is a contradiction.

From the above discussion, $(i, j', k), (i+1, j'', k)\in V$ implies $j' > j''$.
In general, for integers $i_{1} < i_{2}$, $(i_{1}, j_{1}, k), (i_{2}, j_{2}, k)\in V$ implies $j_{1} > j_{2}$.
This property is cyclic by (SL-1).
In particular, for integers $j_{1} < j_{2}$, if $(i_{1}, j_{1}, k), (i_{2}, j_{2}, k)\in V$ then $i_{1} > i_{2}$.

The remaining case is that $j' > j''$.
To see our claim $j'' = j' - 1$, assume that there exists $j_{0}\in \Z$ with $j'' < j_{0} < j'$.
By axiom~(SL-1), take the unique integer $i_{0}\in \Z$ with $(i_{0}, j_{0}, k)\in V$.
Since $j' > j_{0} > j''$ and $(i, j', k), (i_{0}, j_{0}, k), (i+1, j'', k)\in V$, we have $i < i_{0} < i+1$.

Cyclically, one can see that $(i, j, k)\in V$ implies \[
(i, j\pm 1, k\mp 1),
(i\mp 1, j, k\pm 1),
(i\pm 1, j\mp 1, k)\in V.
\]Recalling $(0, 0, 0)\in V$ by (SL-0), we have \[
\bigl\{(i, j, k)\in \Z^{3}\bigm| i + j + k = 0\bigr\}\subset V.
\]Moreover, these two sets are equal by (SL-1).
\end{proof}

%\begin{rem}
%If one drops the uniqueness requirement in (SL-1), and only asks (SL-2), then nonzero sums $i+j+k=n$ can occur only when $\lvert n\rvert=1$ and every adjacent gap in one direction equals to $1$. In that case (cf.\ Example~\ref{ex:Kagome}) there are two disjoint families of tiny triangles indexed by $i+j+k=0$ and by $i+j+k=\pm1$.
%\end{rem}

For a Sturmian lattice $\mathfrak{A}\cup \mathfrak{B}\cup \mathfrak{C}$, we call unbounded strip
\[
\bigl\{(a, b, c)\in \R^{2}\bigm| a(i)\le a\le a(i+1)\bigr\}
\]
between adjacent lines the \emph{corridor} at $a(i)$, and $a(i+1) - a(i)\ge 1$ the \emph{width} of the corridor.
We also define corridors and their widths at $b(j)$ and at $c(k)$ in the same way.

\begin{prop}
\label{prop:mutually-balanced}
For any integers $i$, $j$, and $k$,
\begin{align*}
\bigl| (a(i+1)-a(i)) - (b(j+1)-b(j)) \bigr| &\in \{0, 1\},\\
\bigl| (b(j+1)-b(j)) - (c(k+1)-c(k)) \bigr| &\in \{0, 1\},\\
\bigl| (c(k+1)-c(k)) - (a(i+1)-a(i)) \bigr| &\in \{0, 1\}
\end{align*}
hold.
In other words, for any two families of lines (in any two directions) the differences between the widths of corridors are either equal or differ by $1$.
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
Let $i, j\in \Z$.
By axiom~(SL-1), there exist $k, k'\in \Z$ with $(i+1, j, k), (i, j+1, k')\in V$.
By Proposition~\ref{prop:characterization-V}, we have\[
(i + 1) + j + k =
i + (j + 1) + k' = 0
\]so that $k = k' = -i - j - 1$.
Thus (SL-2) implies
\begin{align*}
a(i+1)+b(j)+c(k) &\in \left\{\frac{1}{2}, -\frac{1}{2}\right\}, \\
a(i)+b(j+1)+c(k) &\in \left\{\frac{1}{2}, -\frac{1}{2}\right\}.
\end{align*}
Subtraction
yields the desired result. The other cases follow similarly.
\end{proof}

\begin{prop}
\label{prop:2-balanced}
For any integers $i$ and $i'$, 
\[
\bigl| (a(i+1)-a(i)) - (a(i'+1)-a(i')) \bigr| \in \{0, 1, 2\},
\]
and similarly
\begin{align*}
\bigl| (b(j+1)-b(j)) - (b(j'+1)-b(j')) \bigr| \in \{0, 1, 2\},\\
\bigl| (c(k+1)-c(k)) - (c(k'+1)-c(k')) \bigr| \in \{0, 1, 2\}.
\end{align*}
In other words, within any one direction, the widths of corridors are either all equal or may differ by at most $2$.
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
This proposition immediately follows from Proposition~\ref{prop:mutually-balanced}.
Indeed, to show that\[
\bigl| (a(i+1)-a(i)) - (a(i'+1)-a(i')) \bigr|\le 2,
\]we may apply the triangle inequality for each left hand of
\begin{align*}
\bigl|(a(i+1) - a(i)) - (b(j+1) - b(j))\bigr|&\le 1,\\
\bigl|(b(j+1) - b(j)) - (a(i'+1) - a(i'))\bigr|&\le 1.
\end{align*}
The other cases follow similarly.
\end{proof}

\begin{prop}\label{prop:two-diff}
Suppose that there are some $i, i'\in \Z$ with \[
\bigl(a(i+1)-a(i)\bigr) - \bigl(a(i'+1)-a(i')\bigr) = 2.
\]Then there exists $\kappa\ge 1$ such that
\begin{align*}
b(j+1) - b(j)&= \kappa,&
c(k + 1) - c(k)&= \kappa
\end{align*}
hold for any $j, k\in \Z$.
%If there exists a direction in which two adjacent spacings differ by 2, then the spacings in each of the other two directions are all equal.
In other words, 
if there exists a direction in which the widths of two corridors differ by 2, then all corridors in the other two directions have the same width.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
Under our assumption, take $\kappa\ge 2$ with
\begin{align*}
a(i+1) - a(i)&= \kappa + 1,&
a(i'+1) - a(i')&= \kappa - 1.
\end{align*}
%and \[
%a(i'+1)-a(i') = \kappa - 1.
%\]
%By Proposition~\ref{prop:mutually-balanced}, the widths of corridors in the other two directions must then necessarily equal to $\kappa$.  
By Proposition~\ref{prop:mutually-balanced} and the former equation, we have\[
\bigl(b(j+1) - b(j)\bigr) - \bigl(a(i+1) - a(i)\bigr)\in \{-1, 0, 1\}
\]for any $j\in \Z$, and hence\[
b(j+1) - b(j)\in \{\kappa, \kappa + 1, \kappa + 2\}.
\]Similarly, the latter $a(i'+1) - a(i'-1) = \kappa - 1$ implies \[
b(j+1) - b(j)\in \{\kappa - 2, \kappa - 1, \kappa\}.
\]These implies $b(j+1) - b(j) = \kappa$.
Similarly, $c(k+1) - c(k) = \kappa$ for $k\in \Z$.
%The same argument applies if the spacing difference of 2 occurs in the $b$- or $c$-direction.
\end{proof}

