\begin{thm}
\label{thm:rho-characterization}
Let $\alpha$ be irrational and a collection $\SL(\kappa, \alpha\mid \bm{\rho})$ of lines continuous.
Then $\SL(\kappa, \alpha\mid \bm{\rho})$ is a (continuous) Sturmian lattice if and only if 
$\rho_0+\rho_1+\rho_2 = 0$.
\end{thm}

\begin{proof}
%In this proof, for simplicity we assume that $n\alpha + \rho_{i}\notin \Z$ for any $i\in \{0, 1, 2\}$ and $n\in \Z$, so that we may put
Suppose $\rho_{0} + \rho_{1} + \rho_{2} = 0$ and let
\begin{align*}
a(i)&= i\kappa + \round{i\alpha + \rho_{0}},&
b(j)&= j\kappa + \round{j\alpha + \rho_{1}},&
c(k)&= k\kappa + \round{k\alpha + \rho_{2}}.
\end{align*}
It suffices to check the axiom~(SL-2) that\[
\bigl|a(i) + b(j) + c(k)\bigr| = \frac{1}{2}
\]for $i + j + k = 0$.
%Since $\alpha$ is irrational, each of the
%fractional sequences 
%$\{i\alpha+\rho_{0}\}_{i\in\Z}$, $\{j\alpha+\rho_{1}\}_{j\in\Z}$, and 
%$\{k\alpha+\rho_{2}\}_{k\in\Z}$ 
%is dense in $[0,1)$.
If $i + j + k = 0$, then 
Lemma~\ref{3Floor} implies (SL-2) almost everywhere and 
therefore it is true everywhere by assumption. 
Converse is shown in the same way. 
\end{proof}

In view of Theorem~\ref{Main0}, two functions determines the rest
almost uniquely.
For a mechanical Sturmian lattice $\SL(\kappa, \alpha\mid \bm{\rho})$, the choice of the remaining line
is possible if and only if $\SL(\kappa, \alpha\mid \bm{\rho})$ is discontinuous.
By Proposition~\ref{prop:discontinuous-SL}, such points form a dense subset of the plane
$\{\bm{\rho}\in \R^{3}\mid \rho_0+\rho_1+\rho_2=0\}$
with $2$-dimensional Lebesgue measure zero.
