\begin{rem}
\label{rem:approx-trigonal}
Theorem~\ref{thm:rho-characterization} also tells us a way to ``approximate'' a mechanical Sturmian lattice by a triangular lattice.
For \[
f(n) = n\kappa + \round{n\alpha + \rho},
\]let \[
\bar{f}(n):= n(\kappa + \alpha) + \rho.
\]The sequence $(\bar{f}(n))_{n\in \Z}$ is an arithmetic progression that approximates $(f(n))_{n\in \Z}$ with error $1/2$.
Indeed, \[
\bar{f}(n + 1) - \bar{f}(n) = \kappa + \alpha
\]and \[
\bigl|f(n) - \bar{f}(n)\bigr|\le \frac{1}{2}
\]for all $n\in \Z$.
Let $\bar{\mathfrak{A}}\cup \bar{\mathfrak{B}}\cup \bar{\mathfrak{C}} = \{\bar{a}(i)\}\cup \{\bar{b}(j)\}\cup \{\bar{c}(k)\}$ with
\begin{align*}
\bar{a}(i)&= i(\kappa + \alpha) + \rho_{0},&
\bar{b}(j)&= j(\kappa + \alpha) + \rho_{1},&
\bar{c}(k)&= k(\kappa + \alpha) + \rho_{2}
\end{align*}
and $\rho_{0} + \rho_{1} + \rho_{2} = 0$.
This is an approximation of mechanical Sturmian lattice $\mathfrak{A}\cup \mathfrak{B}\cup \mathfrak{C}$, and is a triangular lattice.
Indeed, for $(i, j, k)\in V$ the three lines $a = \bar{a}(i)$, $b = \bar{b}(j)$ and $c = \bar{c}(k)$ intersects at $(\bar{a}(i), \bar{b}(j), \bar{c}(k))\in \R^{2}$.
\end{rem}



We also mention the superstructure of mechanical Sturmian lattices, which has a relation with the natural extension\[
\sigma(\kappa, \alpha):= (\kappa^{-1} + \lfloor\alpha^{-1}\rfloor, \alpha^{-1} - \lfloor\alpha^{-1}\rfloor)
\] of the continued fraction map, defined on $X = \bigl((1, \infty)\setminus \Q\bigr)\times \bigl((0, 1)\setminus \Q\bigr)$, see \cite{Dajani-Kraaikamp:02_0, NIT:77, Nakada:81}.
We identify $X$ with $\N^{\Z}$ through $(\kappa, \alpha)\leftrightarrow (a_{n})_{n\in \Z}$, where 
%we write the continued fraction as 
\begin{align*}
\kappa&= %[a_{0}; a_{-1}, a_{-2}, \dots] =
a_{0} +
\frac{1}{a_{-1} +
\cfrac{1}{a_{-2} +
\cfrac{1}{\ddots}}},&
\alpha&= %[a_{1}, a_{2}, \dots] =
\frac{1}{a_{1} +
\cfrac{1}{a_{2} +
\cfrac{1}{\ddots}}},
\end{align*}
are the continued fractions of $\kappa = [a_{0}; a_{-1}, a_{-2}, \dots]$ and $\alpha = [a_{1}, a_{2}, \dots]$.
Then $\sigma$ is equivalent with the left shift on $\N^{\Z}$.
For $n\in \Z$, we write $(\kappa_{n}, \alpha_{n}):= \sigma^{n}(\kappa, \alpha)$.

\begin{definition}
\label{def:superSL}
We define the \emph{super Sturmian lattice $\SL^{n}(\kappa, \alpha\mid \bm{\rho})$ on level $n$ of $\SL(\kappa, \alpha\mid \bm{\rho})$} for $n\in \Z$ by\[
\SL^{n}(\kappa, \alpha\mid \bm{\rho}):=
\SL(\kappa_{n}, \alpha_{n}\mid \bm{\rho}_{n})
\]where $\bm{\rho}_{n} = \alpha_{n-1}^{-1}\bm{\rho}_{n-1}$.
\end{definition}

We give a geometric characterization of the super Sturmian lattice.
As mentioned after Definition~\ref{def:SL-01}, there are two types of corridor widths, $\kappa$ and $\kappa + 1$.
We call the former a \emph{narrow corridor} and the latter a \emph{wide corridor}.
The super Sturmian lattice is depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:superSL}. We shall show that they
appear as the set of middle lines of wide corridors 
as described later in Corollary~\ref{cor:superSL-2D}.
For this purpose, Proposition~\ref{prop:superSL-1D} formally
gives geometric super structure among Sturmian words, using 
the rounding function $\round{{\cdot}}$.

