Note that the centroid of $\ST(i, j, k)$ can be calculated as \[
G(i, j, k):=
\left(\tfrac{2a(i)-b(j)-c(k)}{3},
\tfrac{2b(j)-c(k)-a(i)}{3},
\tfrac{2c(k)-a(i)-b(j)}{3}\right)\in \R^{2}
\]in the triangular coordinate system introduced in \S\ref{SL}.
We define the sets
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{G}_{+}&:= \bigl\{G(i, j, k)\in \R^{2}\bigm| i + j + k = +1\bigr\},\\
\mathcal{G}_{-}&:= \bigl\{G(i, j, k)\in \R^{2}\bigm| i + j + k = -1\bigr\},
\end{align*}
and $\mathcal{G}:= \mathcal{G}_{+}\vee \mathcal{G}_{-}$.
When we identify triangle $\ST(i,j,k)\subset \R^{2}$ with its centroid $G(i,j,k)\in \R^{2}$, we may regard $\mathcal{G}$ as the set of Sturmian triangles.
See also Remark~\ref{rem:VC}.
We also define the subsets
\begin{align*}
\mathcal{G}_{S}&:= \{G(i,j,k)\in \mathcal{G}\mid \text{$G(i,j,k)$ is small}\},\\
\mathcal{G}_{M}&:= \{G(i,j,k)\in \mathcal{G}\mid \text{$G(i,j,k)$ is medium}\},\\
\mathcal{G}_{L}&:= \{G(i,j,k)\in \mathcal{G}\mid \text{$G(i,j,k)$ is large}\}
\end{align*}
of each size of Sturmian triangles.
When we are not concerned with the position of Sturmian triangles, but only with their size, we often write $G_{S}$, $G_{M}$, and $G_{L}$ of size small, medium, and large, respectively.
These symbols are mainly used in \S\ref{Ex}.

For $G(i, j, k)\in \mathcal{G}$, we define three polygons:
\begin{align*}
\VC_{a}(i, j, k)&:= \Conv\bigl(G(i, j', k')\in \mathcal{G}\bigm|
|j - j'| + |k - k'|\le 1\bigr),\\
\VC_{b}(i, j, k)&:= \Conv\bigl(G(i', j, k')\in \mathcal{G}\bigm|
|k - k'| + |i - i'|\le 1\bigr),\\
\VC_{c}(i, j, k)&:= \Conv\bigl(G(i', j', k)\in \mathcal{G}\bigm|
|i - i'| + |j - j'|\le 1\bigr),
\end{align*}
where $\Conv(\cdot)$ denotes the convex hull.
These are indeed $4$-gons: for example, if $i + j + k = -1$ then \[
\VC_{a}(i, j, k) = \Conv\bigl(G(i, j, k), G(i, j+1, k), G(i, j+1, k+1), G(i, j, k+1)\bigr).
\]See Figure~\ref{fig:Voronoi-prop} for a Sturmian triangle and surrounding three Voronoi cells.
We also note that if $i + j + k = 1$, then $\VC_{a}(i, j, k) = \VC_{a}(i, j-1, k-1)$.

