\begin{rem}
\label{rem:VC}
We give a few remarks on Voronoi cells:
\begin{itemize}
\item
The shape and SAB of each Voronoi cell are well-defined, i.e., the shape does not depend on $\alpha$, and the SAB is uniquely determined for each cell.
\item
We naturally identify the Voronoi tiling $\mathcal{VC}$ with the Delone set of centroids of each cell $\VC_{*}(i, j, k)$ (see Figure~\ref{fig:Voronoi-tiling}(b)).
Similarly, we also identify $G(i, j, k)$ with $\ST(i, j, k)$.
\item
There are only three types of Voronoi cells by Proposition~\ref{prop:VC-shape}.
If we denote respectively by $\mathcal{S}$, $\mathcal{M}$, and $\mathcal{L}$ the set of cells $S$'s, $M$'s, and $L$'s in $\mathcal{VC}$, then $\mathcal{VC} = \mathcal{S}\vee \mathcal{M}\vee \mathcal{L}$ holds as a Delone set.
\end{itemize}
\end{rem}

In this paper, we are interested in tilings in which the SABs form a Sturmian lattice.

\medskip
{\bf 
Our goal is to construct a finite set of 
patch-tiles composed of 
three Voronoi cells which is aperiodic.}
\medskip

For our purpose, we assume:

\begin{description}
\item[Matching Rule]
Our tiling must be edge-to-edge and SAB must 
continue across the boundary of patch-tiles to form a line.
\end{description}

The condition \emph{edge-to-edge} of a
polygonal tiling is introduced in \cite{GS}. It means if two polygonal tiles 
share a segment, then it must be an edge of both polygons. This condition implies that
a resulting tiling has FLC, since there are only finite number of patches whose support has a bounded diameter, up to isometry,
To clarify the rule edge-to-edge 
for patch-tiles, we define the edges of a patch-tile.
Let $P = \bigcup_{\lambda}T_{\lambda}$ be a patch-tile and $E_{\lambda} = \{e^{\lambda}_{i}\subset \R^{2}\mid i = 1, \dots, i_{\lambda}\}$ the set of edges of tile $T_{\lambda}$.
An \emph{edge} of patch-tile $P$ is an element of the set
\[
\bigcup_{\lambda}
\{e\in E_{\lambda}\mid e\subset \partial P\}.
\]
For example, we treat a patch-tile shown in Figure~\ref{fig:edge-to-edge} as 
an $8$-gon. 

