\includegraphics[pagebox = artbox, 
width = .8\linewidth, page = 6]
{Example1.pdf}
\caption{Edges of a patch-tile}
\label{fig:edge-to-edge}
\end{figure}

Note that our rule is equivalent to the following.

\begin{description}
\item[Equivalent Matching Rule]
Our tiling must be edge-to-edge and two edges of different colors and vertices of different angles must not overlap.
\end{description}

We check that this matching rule forces SAB to form a Sturmian lattice.
For any point where six $\pi/3$ vertices meet, the colors of the meeting six edges alternate between $\{0, 1\}$ and $\{0', 1'\}$, which means that a tiny triangle must arise uniquely at the intersection of any lines in two directions (see Figure~\ref{fig:VC-vertex-atlas}).
If we take $V$ as the set of $(a, b, c)\in \Z^{3}$ that three lines $a(i)$, $b(j)$, and $c(k)$ give such a tiny triangle, then $V$ satisfies the conditions (SL-1) and (SL-2).
Moreover, $V$ is non-empty; that is, by shifting indices we may assume (SL-0).
The inequalities
\begin{align*}
a(i+1) - a(i)&\ge \kappa,&
b(j+1) - b(j)&\ge \kappa,&
c(k+1) - c(k)&\ge \kappa
\end{align*}
hold when we use the cells $S(\kappa)$, $M(\kappa)$, and $L(\kappa)$.
Thus, our matching rule generates the Sturmian-lattice structure.

