Note that our matching rule could be simplified.
For example, we may define the edges of a patch-tile in the same usual way as for a polygon (we interpret the patch-tile in Figure~\ref{fig:edge-to-edge} as a $6$-gon by removing the three $\pi$ vertices).
Indeed, all vertices of a patch-tile can be obtained by taking the centroids of tiny triangles.
Another possible method is to use \emph{super SAB} (the pattern inherited from $\SL^{1}(\kappa, \alpha)$, see Figure~\ref{fig:super-SAB}) instead of SAB.
Since the super SAB is sparser than the original SAB, the matching rule should be simpler if we use the former.
However, this idea may work (see \S\ref{sec:ex3}) or not (see \S\ref{sec:ex1}).
