\begin{prop}
\label{prop:ND-result}
For a regular Sturmian lattice, the sets $\mathcal{S}$, $\mathcal{M}$, and $\mathcal{L}$ have natural densities
\begin{align*}
\delta(\mathcal{S})&= (1 - \alpha)^{2},&
\delta(\mathcal{M})&= 2\alpha(1 - \alpha),&
\delta(\mathcal{L})&= \alpha^{2}.
\end{align*}
Moreover, they are uniformly spread.
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
We give a rough sketch.
See Appendix~\ref{CalcND} for rigorous discussion.
For each $G(i, j, k)$, three Voronoi cells $\VC_{a}(i, j, k)$, $\VC_{b}(i, j, k)$, and $\VC_{c}(i, j, k)$ form a hexagon that covers point $G(i, j, k)$.
We call it a \emph{hexagonal unit} at $G(i, j, k)$ and consider natural densities for collections of hexagonal units.
It is convenient to consider that a hexagonal unit is at the intersection of two corridors as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:Voronoi-hex-unit}.
Moreover, one can also see that each hexagonal unit consisting of three $S$'s (resp.\ three $L$'s) is at the intersection of two narrow (resp.\ wide) corridors (see Figure~\ref{fig:Voronoi-S-L}), which suggests that the natural densities of $\mathcal{S}$ and $\mathcal{L}$ are $(1 - \alpha)^{2}$ and $\alpha^{2}$, respectively.
\end{proof}

