It is essential to state a BD correspondence between two $S$'s and $L$, and we focus on ``hexagonal units'' again.
As we mentioned in Proposition~\ref{prop:VC-ST}, there is a natural bijection between $\mathcal{G}_{S}$ and the set of small hexagonal units.
In other words, there are $1:3$ BD correspondence between $\mathcal{G}_{S}$ and $\mathcal{S}$.
A similar situation occurs for $L$ so that we may consider a $2 : 1$ BD correspondence between $\mathcal{G}_{S}$ and $\mathcal{G}_{L}$, instead of $\mathcal{S}$ and $\mathcal{L}$.

We also notice that the substitution rule of Sturmian triangles.
Figure~\ref{fig:ex1-AT-substitution} shows the result for Sturmian triangles of each size when the SL substitution is repeated twice.
Recalling Corollary~\ref{cor:superSL-2D}, in the first substitution each line of $\SL^{1}(\kappa, \alpha) = \SL(\kappa_{1}, \alpha)$ is placed in the center of a wide corridor on the original level $0$.
In the second substitution, we next draw $\SL^{2}(\kappa, \alpha) = \SL(\kappa_{2}, \alpha)$ on level $2$.
Of course each line is also the center of a large corridor on level $1$, but for the original level $0$ it becomes a sub-lattice, which consists of lines separating two adjacent small corridors (see Figure~\ref{fig:ex1-superSL}).
%Indeed, one can observe that one (resp.\ two) narrow corridors are lined up in each super narrow (resp.\ wide) corridor.
By counting the number of $G_{S}$, $G_{M}$, and $G_{L}$ after the substitution, each triangle are mapped to 
\begin{align*}
\sigma^{2}(G_{S})&=\03G_{S} + 12G_{M} + \0G_{L},\\
\sigma^{2}(G_{M})&=\06G_{S} + 27G_{M} + 3G_{L},\\
\sigma^{2}(G_{L})&= 10G_{S} + 48G_{M} + 6G_{L}.
\end{align*}
We may ignore $G_{M}$ since it is irrelevant to the BD correspondence.
One can also observe that SL substitution preserves properness.
That is,
\begin{align*}
\sigma^{2}(G_{S}) - G_{S}&\equiv
\0(2G_{S} + G_{L}),\\
\sigma^{2}(G_{M}) - G_{M}&\equiv
3(2G_{S} + G_{L}),\\
\sigma^{2}(G_{L}) - G_{L}&\equiv
5(2G_{S} + G_{L})
\end{align*}
modulo $\Z G_{M}$ are all proper.
In particular, if a patch-tile $T$ is proper, then the image $\sigma^{2}(T)$ is also proper.
This is another perspective on Proposition~\ref{prop:proper-equiv}.

