\subsection{\boldmath Smith Turtle via Voronoi Cells}
\label{sec:ex3}

In this section we try to interpret Smith Turtle in \cite{SMKGS:23_1}, in terms of Voronoi cells.
As we mentioned in Corollary~\ref{Sturm}, the Sturmian lattice that appears in a Turtle tiling has parameters
\begin{align*}
\kappa&= [3; \infty] = 3,&
\alpha&= [\bar{1}] = \frac{-1 + \sqrt{5}}{2}
\end{align*}
and frequency
\[
q = \frac{1}{\kappa + \alpha} =
[3, \bar{1}] = \frac{5 - \sqrt{5}}{10}.
\]

We consider the ``inverse'' of the Voronoi substitution.
Super Voronoi cells on positive level are obtained by joining the original cells, whereas the super Voronoi cells on negative level (we call them \emph{Voronoi sub-cells}) are obtained by splitting the original cells as in Figure~\ref{fig:ex3-sub-Voronoi}.

