\label{fig:ex3-sub-Voronoi}
\end{figure}

Recalling Definition~\ref{def:SL-kappa-infty}, $\SL^{-1}(\kappa, \alpha) = \SL(\infty, q)$ degenerates to a triangular lattice.
In particular, all three types of Voronoi sub-cells are congruent rhombuses with side length $2/3$ and the SAB is drawn as the longer diagonal.
For this reason, degenerate lattice $\SL(\infty, q)$ no longer serve as SABs, and it is better to use super SAB (that is, the original SAB from $\SL(\kappa, \alpha)$).

Figure~\ref{fig:ex3-transformed} is one of suitable examples (the upper part is the original version, and the lower part is the transformed version).
After transforming, three sub-cells still form a ``hexagonal unit'' as shown on the right, which is congruent to the original hexagonal unit.
We denote each size of transformed Voronoi sub-cells by $\tilde{S}_{-1}$, $\tilde{M}_{-1}$, and $\tilde{L}_{-1}$ with a tilde, and similarly we write the set of them as $\tilde{\mathcal{S}}_{-1}$, $\tilde{\mathcal{M}}_{-1}$, and $\tilde{\mathcal{L}}_{-1}$.

